WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:08.070
- Hi everyone.

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:09.060
I'm Saad Omer.

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:11.250
I'm the Director of the Yale
Institute for Global Health

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:14.130
as many of you are likely to know.

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:18.420
I am delighted to welcome
you to the first hybrid event

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:23.420
that we have started doing
in this new academic year

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:29.010
that the Yale Institute for
Global Health has started doing.

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:31.080
We are delighted to be here

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:32.760
at the Yale School of Public Health

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:36.750
and we welcome those who
are joining us online.

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:41.580
I know that a bunch of you
have been joining us online

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:43.353
over the last few years.

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:47.820
In addition to the Yale
Institute for Global Health,

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:52.650
today's event is co-sponsored
by the Walker Fund

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:55.083
at the MacMillan Center at Yale.

00:00:55.980 --> 00:01:00.780
The COVID-19 pandemic has
highlighted the critical need

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:04.080
to better prepare for future pandemics.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:06.060
This is not going to be the last major

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:08.010
infectious disease-related event.

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:09.873
We have already seen monkeypox.

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:14.280
Public health emergencies
pop up with some frequency

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:16.500
and there are estimates
that unfortunately,

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:19.080
the frequency of these kinds of events

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:22.440
is likely to increase.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:26.850
During this event, our panelists
will present and discuss

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:29.160
several themes and several elements

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:32.850
for an effective pandemic response.

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:37.710
We have three very prominent,
thoughtful panelists

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:39.603
and speakers for today's event.

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:43.530
Before moving to their
presentation and our discussion,

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:45.273
I'm gonna introduce them together,

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:48.600
give you a little bit of their background

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:52.230
and then we are gonna go to
their respective presentations

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:55.740
and then the Q&amp;A session where
I'll start with questions,

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:57.600
and then we'll take audience questions,

00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:59.550
both on-site and online.

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:04.550
So, Dr. Jennifer Nuzzo
is the Inaugural Director

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:07.590
of the Pandemic Center at Brown University

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:08.423
School of Public Health.

00:02:08.423 --> 00:02:12.510
Before I became the
Inaugural Director of YIGH,

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:16.710
I used to imagine that people
just come into their centers

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:19.020
on day one and cut a ribbon or something,

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:21.120
that's how they become
inaugural directors.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:22.923
I have no such illusion anymore.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:28.590
But the Center has already done
a lot of significant things

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:29.423
and

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:33.330
is becoming fairly prominent

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:35.850
in the discourse around
public health preparedness.

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:38.730
She's also a Senior
Fellow for Global Health

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:42.120
at the Council on Foreign Relations.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:44.790
She's an epidemiologist by training.

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:48.510
Her work focuses on
global health security,

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:50.640
public health preparedness and response.

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:55.080
And I guess, if I can
sort of share my bias,

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:58.110
most importantly, on
health systems resilience.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:02:59.400
So, we are public health people,

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:03.240
we go for primary prevention if possible.

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:07.983
So, creating a resilient health
system is in that category.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:13.530
Dr. Nuzzo regularly
engages with the media,

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:17.130
both electronic and print
media, to educate and inform

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:20.793
about emerging health security trends.

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:26.490
Dr. Wilmot James is a
former Member of Parliament

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:30.780
in South Africa and ranking
opposition spokesperson

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:31.800
on health.

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:34.380
Currently, he's a Senior
Researcher Scholar

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:38.100
at the Institute for Social and
Economic Research and Policy

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:41.790
at Columbia University, just
a small train ride away,

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:45.600
where he leads the Center
for Pandemic Research.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:49.860
He conducts research on pandemic
response and biosecurity

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:51.750
and convenes, with some frequency,

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:55.080
high-level meetings on planetary threats.

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:58.140
He's also an Honorary
Professor of Global Health

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:01.653
at University of
Witwatersrand in Johannesburg.

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:08.180
Beth Cameron is a Professor of
Practice of Health Services,

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:12.240
Policy and Practice at Brown University.

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:15.960
Dr. Cameron is a national
expert in health security,

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:18.960
pandemic preparedness,
biosecurity, biodefense,

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:21.510
and bioterrorism.

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:25.560
She has worked at the highest
level of the government,

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:30.430
including serving as the special
assistant to the President

00:04:31.307 --> 00:04:32.140
and Senior Director

00:04:32.140 --> 00:04:33.930
for Global Health Security and Biodefense

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:37.050
for the White House
National Security Council.

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:39.510
She also serves as a senior advisor

00:04:39.510 --> 00:04:44.510
to the U.S. Agency for
International Development, USAID,

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:49.080
but here, is not
representing any government,

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:50.130
or government agency.

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:55.130
She's here as a professor and
in her own personal capacity

00:04:55.650 --> 00:04:58.230
as a key opinion leader in this area.

00:04:58.230 --> 00:05:01.983
So, we are honored to have
all three of you here today.

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:05.940
So, we...

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:08.100
For today's event, each of our panelists

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:10.290
will do a short presentation

00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:12.870
followed by one, or two questions,

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:15.690
so, after the presentation is complete.

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:18.480
Actually, I would suggest that
you restrict those questions

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:22.260
to clarifying questions, and
any substantive questions,

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:26.670
we will get to them after
the initial presentation.

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:30.180
So, if you want any clarification,
feel free to follow up,

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:32.610
but if you have more
substantive questions,

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:34.293
we'll do that in the panel.

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:38.280
Obviously, for those on Zoom,

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:40.833
will also have an
opportunity to ask questions.

00:05:41.670 --> 00:05:44.700
With that, I would like
to welcome Dr. Nuzzo

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:47.598
to deliver the presentation.
(pages rustling)

00:05:47.598 --> 00:05:50.765
(audience applauding)

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:53.273
- Thank you so much.

00:05:53.273 --> 00:05:54.540
Thanks for that kind introduction

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:55.890
and thanks for the invitation.

00:05:55.890 --> 00:05:58.560
It's really such a pleasure to be here,

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:01.560
to learn more about the work
of people I admire so greatly,

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:03.960
like yourself, Saad, and your colleagues,

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:04.950
but also to meet with students.

00:06:04.950 --> 00:06:06.120
We had great conversations
(object thudding)

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:07.680
today.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:10.410
If we advance to the next
slide, we're gonna spend today

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:13.530
talking about how to better prepare

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:14.973
for these sorts of events.

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:17.010
That's a huge topic,

00:06:17.010 --> 00:06:18.900
but I'm just gonna take two small pieces,

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:20.910
which is thinking about surveillance

00:06:20.910 --> 00:06:22.950
and the role of surveillance
and how we can improve it

00:06:22.950 --> 00:06:24.690
as well as the workforce,

00:06:24.690 --> 00:06:27.240
the workforce to support
surveillance, but to some extent,

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:30.213
the broader public health workforce.

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:34.740
Prior to coming to Brown,
while I was at Johns Hopkins,

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:37.320
I had the privilege to be part of the team

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:40.680
behind the Johns Hopkins'
coronavirus map, which was started

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:44.010
by my Hopkins colleague,
Dr. Lauren Gardner.

00:06:44.010 --> 00:06:46.290
I'm showing a screenshot of the map

00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:48.870
from actually Friday of last week.

00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:51.870
So, these are somewhat up-to-date numbers,

00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:54.120
but I'm showing this really
as a bit of an allegory

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:55.230
for how we should think about

00:06:55.230 --> 00:06:57.090
improving surveillance and data.

00:06:57.090 --> 00:06:58.380
The first thing I wanna point out,

00:06:58.380 --> 00:06:59.730
is that this map was created
(Jennifer chuckles)

00:06:59.730 --> 00:07:01.110
by people outside of government

00:07:01.110 --> 00:07:02.820
because there was nothing like it

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:05.160
and there was very much
a need to stay up-to-date

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:06.930
with the case numbers,
where they were occurring,

00:07:06.930 --> 00:07:08.340
and to analyze those case numbers

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:10.410
in the context of other factors.

00:07:10.410 --> 00:07:14.250
So, that was clearly a
need that, in my mind,

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:16.560
there is now at least
a mechanism to address

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:19.350
with the creation of the WHO Pandemic Hub,

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:21.270
but still, I think a long
way to go, to make sure

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:24.420
that we have, in future
events, something like this.

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:26.790
This was an extraordinary
resource intensive effort,

00:07:26.790 --> 00:07:28.410
very expensive effort.

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:32.520
I'm very proud of the work
that it did, but it also,

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:34.440
I think, represented just a small piece

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:37.830
of the challenge of fixing surveillance

00:07:37.830 --> 00:07:40.680
for these sorts of global emergencies.

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:43.560
Part of why we need to fix
this is not just to get

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:46.050
the up-to-date picture,
the dashboard that tells us

00:07:46.050 --> 00:07:48.480
what's going on now and to
help us analyze these numbers

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:52.440
so that we can understand how
best to target our resources,

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:56.580
but we also need to understand
the context by which the data

00:07:56.580 --> 00:07:59.340
that populate these sorts
of dashboards are generated.

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:01.140
And that's the real
piece that I don't think

00:08:01.140 --> 00:08:03.360
that we're spending
enough time trying to fix.

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:05.310
If you could advance to the next slide.

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:08.790
I'm just gonna show you a
screenshot from one day.

00:08:08.790 --> 00:08:10.440
Actually, this graph doesn't appear

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:12.360
on the dashboard anymore, but it used to,

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:15.330
and it was the daily case
numbers being reported each day

00:08:15.330 --> 00:08:16.380
across the globe.

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:18.900
As you can see from this
graph, there's one day

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:20.820
that's not (chuckles) like the others.

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:22.860
There was one day, and you
can imagine on that day

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:24.877
when we got those case
numbers, we thought,

00:08:24.877 --> 00:08:25.980
"What's going on?

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:27.697
Like, "This is an error.

00:08:27.697 --> 00:08:29.167
"Is something else happening?

00:08:29.167 --> 00:08:30.840
"What is going on?"

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:34.140
It wasn't until you kind of
parse, you look at the data

00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:36.150
and you realize it's all
coming from one country,

00:08:36.150 --> 00:08:37.650
and then you look in the news,

00:08:37.650 --> 00:08:38.850
and you realize what's happening,

00:08:38.850 --> 00:08:42.750
which is that this was a day
where a single country decided

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:44.403
to change its case definition.

00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:49.200
So, one country's policy
decisions can greatly change

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:52.890
our understanding of how
the pandemic is unfolding

00:08:52.890 --> 00:08:54.480
and what it means for
the global community.

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:57.360
And the truth is, we don't have
the standardized definitions

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:01.140
so that all countries are
finding and reporting cases

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:02.190
in the same way.

00:09:02.190 --> 00:09:04.230
So, what we have here,
is really a hodgepodge

00:09:04.230 --> 00:09:06.480
based on what countries decide to do.

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:07.470
This is one of the challenges

00:09:07.470 --> 00:09:09.000
when we talk about global surveillance,

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:11.070
which is how do we
actually get a meaningful,

00:09:11.070 --> 00:09:13.620
common global picture of what's happening,

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:17.910
absent standards for how
we collect these data,

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:21.420
how hard we look for these
data, and how we report them.

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:22.820
If you go to the next slide.

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:26.310
This is further complicated.

00:09:26.310 --> 00:09:28.410
So, the case definition one is probably

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:32.070
an easier problem to solve,
but (chuckles) what underpins

00:09:32.070 --> 00:09:35.580
our abilities to find cases
is a much harder thing

00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:37.470
that we also need to invest in.

00:09:37.470 --> 00:09:40.980
And that's, countries
have different capacities

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:43.170
to go out and look for cases.

00:09:43.170 --> 00:09:46.410
This is a graph that's not up-to-date,

00:09:46.410 --> 00:09:50.880
but it was taken at a point in
time showing test positivity

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:52.230
from different countries.

00:09:52.230 --> 00:09:54.480
As you can see, there's a huge range.

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:57.210
There are some countries, like
Mexico's up here on the top,

00:09:57.210 --> 00:09:59.310
that had very high test positivity

00:09:59.310 --> 00:10:01.560
throughout the entire
duration of the pandemic.

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:02.760
What that meant

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:05.310
is that they were not doing
a whole lot of testing.

00:10:05.310 --> 00:10:07.440
So, the people who were
getting tested were the people

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:12.390
who were highly probable of
having the SARS-CoV-2 infection

00:10:12.390 --> 00:10:14.400
that causes COVID-19.

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:17.730
So, that means that broad
testing in the community,

00:10:17.730 --> 00:10:19.770
testing people who are asymptomatic,

00:10:19.770 --> 00:10:22.020
was largely not happening.

00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:22.860
This isn't indeed true.

00:10:22.860 --> 00:10:25.410
When you look at different
countries' policies

00:10:25.410 --> 00:10:27.180
and different countries' practices,

00:10:27.180 --> 00:10:30.600
you see a wide range in terms
of what countries were doing.

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:33.480
One of the things we found
in looking at these data

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:37.740
was that many countries were largely,

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:41.640
throughout much of the
pandemic, only testing travelers

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:44.910
because travelers paid for the tests.

00:10:44.910 --> 00:10:46.740
What that meant is the
positive test results

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:48.810
were largely coming from people

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:53.040
who were from outside the
country, probably lower risk

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:55.200
because they were probably
wealthy and traveling

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:57.570
and not necessarily then reflective

00:10:57.570 --> 00:11:00.840
of what was happening in country
in terms of transmission.

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:04.740
So, if that's what we're
rolling up into our national,

00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:07.500
or our global surveillance
picture, then we're not getting

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:08.400
a real sense
(Jennifer chuckles)

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:10.590
of what is happening within countries.

00:11:10.590 --> 00:11:13.500
So, beware when you see analyses that say

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:15.210
this country did better than this country

00:11:15.210 --> 00:11:17.910
based on the data that
these countries reported,

00:11:17.910 --> 00:11:19.740
recognizing that the processes

00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:23.130
by which those data are generated
are very, very different

00:11:23.130 --> 00:11:27.747
and do not in any way conclude
(chuckles) that capacities

00:11:29.011 --> 00:11:30.060
(siren wailing)
may not be relevant

00:11:30.060 --> 00:11:33.360
to those analyses because
capacities are what countries use

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:36.180
to not only go out and diagnose cases,

00:11:36.180 --> 00:11:39.000
but also to count deaths and
to determine cause of death.

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:41.190
And if we wanna get a
standardized picture,

00:11:41.190 --> 00:11:43.560
we need to make sure, we need
to level the playing field

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:45.780
and make sure all countries
have the same capacities

00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:48.600
and equal probability of
finding cases, finding deaths,

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:52.110
determining cause of deaths,
and being able to report them.

00:11:52.110 --> 00:11:52.943
Next slide.

00:11:55.260 --> 00:11:58.380
So, that's just a very
brief snip on the data side.

00:11:58.380 --> 00:12:00.330
There's so much more I could
say about data improvements,

00:12:00.330 --> 00:12:02.280
but I wanna save time for the discussion.

00:12:02.280 --> 00:12:03.720
So, I'll just kind of pause there

00:12:03.720 --> 00:12:05.100
and then turn to
(Jennifer chuckles)

00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:07.980
the workforce because I
do think that sometimes

00:12:07.980 --> 00:12:09.900
we spend a lot of time talking about data

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:13.620
as though it's an end
point, as opposed to a tool,

00:12:13.620 --> 00:12:17.130
an information input to a
process that's supposed to end

00:12:17.130 --> 00:12:20.163
with a public health action
taken to make people safer.

00:12:21.300 --> 00:12:24.720
What is needed is not just
a data point, a 1 and a 0

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:29.340
that your computer generates,
but people to gather the data,

00:12:29.340 --> 00:12:31.500
to analyze the data, to act upon the data,

00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:33.990
and then to take action

00:12:33.990 --> 00:12:36.630
depending on where the data points us.

00:12:36.630 --> 00:12:38.790
There are many different types
of workforce that really,

00:12:38.790 --> 00:12:42.180
I think, are essential for this work,

00:12:42.180 --> 00:12:43.620
but I'll just focus on one

00:12:43.620 --> 00:12:45.780
and I'll talk about the
epidemiology workforce.

00:12:45.780 --> 00:12:47.630
What I'm showing here is a graph from

00:12:48.990 --> 00:12:52.050
the most recent version of the
Global Health Security Index,

00:12:52.050 --> 00:12:55.470
which we published in the end of 2021.

00:12:55.470 --> 00:12:59.670
This shows just a snapshot of
the epidemiologic workforce.

00:12:59.670 --> 00:13:02.520
Clearly, you can see again,
lots of heterogeneity

00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:04.020
across the globe in terms of

00:13:04.020 --> 00:13:07.110
how many epidemiologists per
capita exist in countries

00:13:07.110 --> 00:13:08.284
and I think
(sirens wailing)

00:13:08.284 --> 00:13:09.990
it really speaks to the
continued work to make sure

00:13:09.990 --> 00:13:14.550
we develop, train, and retain
the epidemiologic workforce.

00:13:14.550 --> 00:13:16.890
And I put some emphasis
on that retain part

00:13:16.890 --> 00:13:20.010
because I am deeply worried
about that going forward.

00:13:20.010 --> 00:13:21.360
I am deeply worried (chuckles).

00:13:21.360 --> 00:13:23.700
The United State's
colored in relatively dark

00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:24.600
compared to other countries,

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:27.240
meaning we tend to have more
epidemiologists per capita

00:13:27.240 --> 00:13:29.040
as compared to other countries,

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:30.120
but if you've talked to anybody

00:13:30.120 --> 00:13:32.250
who's worked in public health departments,

00:13:32.250 --> 00:13:35.070
you have learned that
there's been a hemorrhaging

00:13:35.070 --> 00:13:38.070
of qualified, experienced
public health workers,

00:13:38.070 --> 00:13:42.150
in part because they're burnt
out, they feel threatened,

00:13:42.150 --> 00:13:43.080
they feel like

00:13:43.080 --> 00:13:45.483
there's a better career
path for them elsewhere.

00:13:46.740 --> 00:13:48.720
This is likely not unique
to the United States.

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:50.130
We've seen this in many contexts

00:13:50.130 --> 00:13:51.660
and so, I think we've really have to worry

00:13:51.660 --> 00:13:53.760
about the sustainability
of these workforces

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:56.220
and making sure we not only train them,

00:13:56.220 --> 00:13:58.230
but we also retain them.

00:13:58.230 --> 00:13:59.063
Next slide.

00:14:02.820 --> 00:14:04.170
I do think though,

00:14:04.170 --> 00:14:06.810
that it's not just about
training epidemiologists.

00:14:06.810 --> 00:14:09.060
It's not just training
community health workers,

00:14:09.060 --> 00:14:12.180
which I frankly think is
one area of deep deficiency

00:14:12.180 --> 00:14:15.600
for which, if we were gonna
put efforts into any one place,

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:17.640
in my view, would probably be that.

00:14:17.640 --> 00:14:20.040
Saad, to your point about
health systems resilience,

00:14:20.040 --> 00:14:21.930
I think that's one key lesson

00:14:21.930 --> 00:14:23.460
from our work in health systems resilience

00:14:23.460 --> 00:14:26.970
about the importance and unmet
needs in terms of shoring up

00:14:26.970 --> 00:14:28.890
the community health workforce.

00:14:28.890 --> 00:14:32.430
But it's not just about
the technical skills.

00:14:32.430 --> 00:14:35.160
It's not just about putting
people in these positions.

00:14:35.160 --> 00:14:38.400
It's also about training
them and empowering them

00:14:38.400 --> 00:14:41.190
to make decisions and to
act upon the information

00:14:41.190 --> 00:14:44.167
that they have at hand to
say, "We need to do something

00:14:44.167 --> 00:14:46.327
"and here's what we need
to do based on my training,

00:14:46.327 --> 00:14:47.437
"based on my experiences,

00:14:47.437 --> 00:14:50.130
"based on what I'm seeing in the data."

00:14:50.130 --> 00:14:52.350
Again, data are not the end point.

00:14:52.350 --> 00:14:54.240
Data, they're the start
of the process. (chuckles)

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:56.490
They're the information we use to act.

00:14:56.490 --> 00:15:01.410
But what we have seen is
really a lack of empowerment

00:15:01.410 --> 00:15:03.750
of a public health
workforce to be able to act

00:15:03.750 --> 00:15:05.940
and to be able to act swiftly

00:15:05.940 --> 00:15:08.760
such that we respond to events that occur

00:15:08.760 --> 00:15:10.560
with the possibility
that they could be worse

00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:12.390
than they might seem right now.

00:15:12.390 --> 00:15:16.350
And let me tell you, seeing the monkeypox,

00:15:16.350 --> 00:15:19.320
I'll just call it a debacle,
play out in the United States,

00:15:19.320 --> 00:15:22.080
on the heels of a COVID debacle,

00:15:22.080 --> 00:15:27.080
where delays resulted in
costs to threats to life,

00:15:28.140 --> 00:15:29.520
seeing that yet again play out

00:15:29.520 --> 00:15:31.140
in terms of our response to monkeypox,

00:15:31.140 --> 00:15:34.260
a virus for which we had
more tools and frankly,

00:15:34.260 --> 00:15:38.010
a better leg-up than we did for COVID,

00:15:38.010 --> 00:15:42.090
I think we have to take a hard
look at how we are training

00:15:42.090 --> 00:15:45.390
and empowering public health
workers to act on situations

00:15:45.390 --> 00:15:47.100
such that we can get ahead of them

00:15:47.100 --> 00:15:48.750
instead of constantly looking backwards

00:15:48.750 --> 00:15:52.890
and leading sort of based on
our retrospective understanding

00:15:52.890 --> 00:15:53.890
of what's happening.

00:15:55.080 --> 00:15:55.913
Next slide.

00:15:58.860 --> 00:16:00.930
I also think, in addition
to having the data

00:16:00.930 --> 00:16:04.260
and having the workforce,
and obviously we can look at

00:16:04.260 --> 00:16:06.300
what's happening in
events that are happening,

00:16:06.300 --> 00:16:09.210
but in the interim, we need to practice.

00:16:09.210 --> 00:16:12.330
We need to practice,
not just as a workforce

00:16:12.330 --> 00:16:15.930
with the plans that we
have, but also as societies.

00:16:15.930 --> 00:16:17.610
I have spent a lot of time thinking

00:16:17.610 --> 00:16:20.340
about how pandemic threats relates

00:16:20.340 --> 00:16:22.350
to other hazards in our life.

00:16:22.350 --> 00:16:24.690
I spent a lot of time thinking
about how it relates to

00:16:24.690 --> 00:16:26.520
how we think about fires.

00:16:26.520 --> 00:16:30.930
What's clear is that when
you have a recurring hazard,

00:16:30.930 --> 00:16:33.270
you don't just need the
capacities to deal with them.

00:16:33.270 --> 00:16:36.570
You also need to practice
what you do when it happens.

00:16:36.570 --> 00:16:38.820
That is something where I
think we are quite deficient

00:16:38.820 --> 00:16:42.450
and there are, I think, more
opportunities to practice.

00:16:42.450 --> 00:16:44.880
Prior to the pandemic,
we did a study looking at

00:16:44.880 --> 00:16:48.750
how Taiwan conducted, every single year,

00:16:48.750 --> 00:16:50.790
mass vaccination exercises.

00:16:50.790 --> 00:16:52.470
They did it during the flu season.

00:16:52.470 --> 00:16:56.010
They did it so that they could
boost uptake of flu vaccines

00:16:56.010 --> 00:16:57.360
among the most vulnerable,

00:16:57.360 --> 00:16:59.400
but they also did it so
that they could practice

00:16:59.400 --> 00:17:00.750
what they would do in a pandemic.

00:17:00.750 --> 00:17:03.030
So, not only could they test their systems

00:17:03.030 --> 00:17:06.360
and their personnel, but
that the public would know

00:17:06.360 --> 00:17:09.360
where to go to get a vaccine,
so that in an emergency

00:17:09.360 --> 00:17:11.010
it wouldn't feel like this weird thing

00:17:11.010 --> 00:17:12.480
that showed up out of nowhere.

00:17:12.480 --> 00:17:13.800
That it was the thing that you do,

00:17:13.800 --> 00:17:16.170
just like we do regular fire drills

00:17:16.170 --> 00:17:19.080
so that when the alarms go off for real,

00:17:19.080 --> 00:17:23.220
we don't spend critical time
trying to convince ourselves

00:17:23.220 --> 00:17:24.210
it's not really happening.

00:17:24.210 --> 00:17:26.310
We just act because it feels familiar.

00:17:26.310 --> 00:17:27.600
We rely on that muscle memory.

00:17:27.600 --> 00:17:30.840
And I think we need to do
more of that in public health.

00:17:30.840 --> 00:17:31.673
Next slide.

00:17:34.440 --> 00:17:37.317
Then finally, I talked
about this sustainment issue

00:17:37.317 --> 00:17:39.930
and the point that we can't just buy

00:17:39.930 --> 00:17:43.140
these single-use solutions
every time we have an emergency.

00:17:43.140 --> 00:17:46.500
We can't just take a whole lot of money,

00:17:46.500 --> 00:17:48.810
try to hire a whole
lot of contract workers

00:17:48.810 --> 00:17:51.390
and then as soon as the
case numbers come down

00:17:51.390 --> 00:17:53.580
and the funding lapses, we let them all go

00:17:53.580 --> 00:17:55.020
and then we find ourselves scrambling

00:17:55.020 --> 00:17:56.130
when the next event happens.

00:17:56.130 --> 00:17:57.870
We have to sustain it.

00:17:57.870 --> 00:17:59.790
This is, I have data here
from the United States,

00:17:59.790 --> 00:18:01.140
but I think this is playing out

00:18:01.140 --> 00:18:03.030
in many, many places across the globe

00:18:03.030 --> 00:18:06.300
where we have that initial
emergency response,

00:18:06.300 --> 00:18:09.510
and then we get tired and we
move on to other priorities.

00:18:09.510 --> 00:18:13.650
So, this is just what public
health preparedness looked like

00:18:13.650 --> 00:18:17.827
in the early 2001, 2002, 20003.

00:18:17.827 --> 00:18:21.150
So, 2001, some of you may
be too young to remember,

00:18:21.150 --> 00:18:23.580
but the nation experienced a real shock

00:18:23.580 --> 00:18:25.350
in terms of the events of September 11th

00:18:25.350 --> 00:18:27.810
and a real commitment
to never again make sure

00:18:27.810 --> 00:18:31.410
that we are unprepared for
public health emergencies.

00:18:31.410 --> 00:18:34.470
So, there was a real increase
in the amount of funding

00:18:34.470 --> 00:18:36.510
and that was sustained for quite a while

00:18:36.510 --> 00:18:39.090
and the peak was around
2005 where, I would say,

00:18:39.090 --> 00:18:40.080
that was probably the time

00:18:40.080 --> 00:18:42.570
when our preparedness
programs were the strongest.

00:18:42.570 --> 00:18:44.470
But then, if you go to the next slide,

00:18:45.420 --> 00:18:48.573
you'll see over time,
that commitment lapsed.

00:18:50.160 --> 00:18:53.520
These data cut off before, I think,

00:18:53.520 --> 00:18:56.220
the worst of what we saw
happen in public health,

00:18:56.220 --> 00:18:59.820
which was the economic downturn of 2008

00:18:59.820 --> 00:19:02.730
where we know that there
was a hemorrhaging of staff

00:19:02.730 --> 00:19:03.600
from health departments

00:19:03.600 --> 00:19:06.450
as state budgets basically contracted.

00:19:06.450 --> 00:19:07.283
We saw this play out

00:19:07.283 --> 00:19:09.930
in other places around the world as well.

00:19:09.930 --> 00:19:12.360
2008 Represented a real departure point

00:19:12.360 --> 00:19:14.010
in terms of our preparedness programs

00:19:14.010 --> 00:19:16.530
and it was also accelerated by the fact

00:19:16.530 --> 00:19:19.010
that national funding for
these programs dried up

00:19:19.010 --> 00:19:22.380
at the same time that the
economic crisis really strained

00:19:22.380 --> 00:19:24.270
local budgets.

00:19:24.270 --> 00:19:25.103
Next slide.

00:19:27.870 --> 00:19:29.430
This has consequences.

00:19:29.430 --> 00:19:30.900
This has real consequences.
(Jennifer chuckles)

00:19:30.900 --> 00:19:33.630
This boom-and-bust cycle
has real consequences.

00:19:33.630 --> 00:19:35.280
One of them was, just before the pandemic,

00:19:35.280 --> 00:19:37.950
we interviewed health departments
around the United States,

00:19:37.950 --> 00:19:40.200
asking them about their
response to the measles outbreak

00:19:40.200 --> 00:19:42.600
that were happening before the pandemic.

00:19:42.600 --> 00:19:44.010
One of the questions we asked them about,

00:19:44.010 --> 00:19:46.230
was contact tracing, the extent
to which they were employing

00:19:46.230 --> 00:19:48.990
contact tracing in responding
to these measles outbreaks.

00:19:48.990 --> 00:19:50.240
If you don't know anything about measles,

00:19:50.240 --> 00:19:52.380
it is the clearest case for the need

00:19:52.380 --> 00:19:53.820
to use contact tracing.
(Jennifer chuckles)

00:19:53.820 --> 00:19:56.310
Measles is a highly transmissible virus

00:19:56.310 --> 00:19:59.480
and wanting to put a
large circle around it

00:19:59.480 --> 00:20:00.930
is really quite important.

00:20:00.930 --> 00:20:02.520
But what we found, is that
most health departments

00:20:02.520 --> 00:20:04.470
were not using contact tracing.

00:20:04.470 --> 00:20:06.930
They didn't have the workforce
to do contact tracing.

00:20:06.930 --> 00:20:08.550
So, when they had measles outbreaks,

00:20:08.550 --> 00:20:09.600
basically all they were doing

00:20:09.600 --> 00:20:11.497
was kind of putting up a notice saying,

00:20:11.497 --> 00:20:13.507
"If you were in these
places during these times,

00:20:13.507 --> 00:20:14.827
"you may have been exposed to measles.

00:20:14.827 --> 00:20:16.437
"Call your healthcare provider."

00:20:17.310 --> 00:20:20.490
Great, if you tell the
healthcare provider what to say.

00:20:20.490 --> 00:20:23.340
Great, if you actually have a
healthcare provider to go to.

00:20:23.340 --> 00:20:24.630
And we certainly know that

00:20:24.630 --> 00:20:25.680
both of those situations
(Jennifer chuckles)

00:20:25.680 --> 00:20:27.840
are not necessarily true.

00:20:27.840 --> 00:20:31.290
We saw a degradation in capacity
just before the pandemic

00:20:31.290 --> 00:20:33.750
and the consequences of that
were very clear in the pandemic

00:20:33.750 --> 00:20:35.940
when we needed an emergency supplemental

00:20:35.940 --> 00:20:38.190
to suddenly hire all of
these contract workers

00:20:38.190 --> 00:20:39.990
to do the work of contact tracing,

00:20:39.990 --> 00:20:41.610
which if you know anything
about contact tracing,

00:20:41.610 --> 00:20:44.370
is more like an art than
a science and really hard

00:20:44.370 --> 00:20:47.343
to just drop in place and have
it be effective overnight.

00:20:48.330 --> 00:20:49.163
Next slide.

00:20:50.730 --> 00:20:53.100
This is really my last content
slide, which is to say,

00:20:53.100 --> 00:20:55.290
despite what you may have heard last night

00:20:55.290 --> 00:20:58.792
about the pandemic being over,
I don't think that it is,

00:20:58.792 --> 00:21:00.690
(chuckles) but even if you think

00:21:00.690 --> 00:21:02.370
that the emergency of COVID is over,

00:21:02.370 --> 00:21:04.980
even if you think that 500+ deaths a day

00:21:04.980 --> 00:21:09.390
is no longer an emergency,
I don't, but if you do,

00:21:09.390 --> 00:21:12.420
you should not take
from the ending of COVID

00:21:12.420 --> 00:21:14.670
that this is a once in a century crisis,

00:21:14.670 --> 00:21:17.160
and we have 99 more years of safety.

00:21:17.160 --> 00:21:18.990
The fact is and, Saad, you alluded to this

00:21:18.990 --> 00:21:19.890
in your opening remarks,

00:21:19.890 --> 00:21:22.650
the frequency with which we are
having these sorts of events

00:21:22.650 --> 00:21:23.760
is increasing.

00:21:23.760 --> 00:21:25.230
There will be more.

00:21:25.230 --> 00:21:27.450
So, these investments in
surveillance and workforce,

00:21:27.450 --> 00:21:30.270
and many, many, many other
components of preparedness

00:21:30.270 --> 00:21:33.420
have to be viewed as fixed costs,

00:21:33.420 --> 00:21:37.620
maintaining an infrastructure
that appropriately addresses

00:21:37.620 --> 00:21:39.150
the hazards of our times,

00:21:39.150 --> 00:21:41.520
which is infectious disease emergencies.

00:21:41.520 --> 00:21:42.903
So, with that, thank you.

00:21:44.351 --> 00:21:47.518
(audience applauding)

00:21:49.650 --> 00:21:51.390
- [Saad] Take a couple
of clarifying questions.

00:21:51.390 --> 00:21:54.090
- Any clarifying questions? (chuckles)

00:21:54.090 --> 00:21:55.410
Okay.

00:21:55.410 --> 00:21:56.820
Yeah.

00:21:56.820 --> 00:21:58.800
- [Audience Member] Thank you
so much for the presentation.

00:21:58.800 --> 00:22:03.060
I worked on one of the first
federal vaccination sites

00:22:03.060 --> 00:22:04.421
in Oakland, one of those mass

00:22:04.421 --> 00:22:05.520
(object scraping)
vaccine sites,

00:22:05.520 --> 00:22:08.970
and we really were building
stuff from scratch.

00:22:08.970 --> 00:22:11.850
I'm really wondering,
are there other reasons

00:22:11.850 --> 00:22:13.620
as to why public health departments

00:22:13.620 --> 00:22:17.280
are notoriously underfunded
for pandemic operations?

00:22:17.280 --> 00:22:20.760
Are they allocating
budgets to other things?

00:22:20.760 --> 00:22:22.410
Are there other reasons for that?

00:22:23.310 --> 00:22:24.393
- So, quick response.

00:22:27.030 --> 00:22:28.860
What we found... We did another
study actually looking at

00:22:28.860 --> 00:22:30.960
mass vaccination capabilities
at health departments

00:22:30.960 --> 00:22:33.510
and one of the things we found is that

00:22:33.510 --> 00:22:36.330
health departments tended
to work on building plans

00:22:36.330 --> 00:22:38.460
to hand out pills because
they were working around

00:22:38.460 --> 00:22:40.620
an anthrax response scenario

00:22:40.620 --> 00:22:43.020
rather than a vaccination
strategy scenario,

00:22:43.020 --> 00:22:44.820
which is a very, very different one.

00:22:44.820 --> 00:22:46.560
In part because of not enough resources

00:22:46.560 --> 00:22:48.210
to do those sorts of plans,

00:22:48.210 --> 00:22:52.680
but also they had to do
mass vaccination efforts

00:22:52.680 --> 00:22:55.260
in some part during the 2009 pandemic.

00:22:55.260 --> 00:22:58.687
And we very frequently heard
from health departments,

00:22:58.687 --> 00:23:01.327
"Yeah, we used to have plans for 2009,

00:23:01.327 --> 00:23:02.977
"but the person who worked on those plans

00:23:02.977 --> 00:23:04.863
"is no longer here."

00:23:04.863 --> 00:23:05.696
- [Audience Member] Okay.

00:23:05.696 --> 00:23:06.529
- So.

00:23:09.464 --> 00:23:10.500
(Saad speaking softly)
- Yeah.

00:23:10.500 --> 00:23:13.860
- [Audience Member] Why is
infectious disease frequency

00:23:13.860 --> 00:23:15.093
increasing, outbreaks?

00:23:15.930 --> 00:23:19.370
- Yeah, (sighs) it's sort of
the conditions of our times

00:23:19.370 --> 00:23:20.580
in the globe.

00:23:20.580 --> 00:23:22.893
We are much more connected
than we ever were,

00:23:23.730 --> 00:23:25.380
so we do a lot more travel than we did.

00:23:25.380 --> 00:23:28.260
So, that just gives the
ability for people to mix

00:23:28.260 --> 00:23:30.570
in ways they didn't mix and
to bring things with them.

00:23:30.570 --> 00:23:34.230
Also, we are encroaching into
areas that put us in contact

00:23:34.230 --> 00:23:37.260
with pathogens that we may
not have had contact with.

00:23:37.260 --> 00:23:40.560
Environmental drivers of
pathogen emergence and spillover

00:23:40.560 --> 00:23:42.660
is increasing and then we, as a society,

00:23:42.660 --> 00:23:44.340
are more vulnerable to these things.

00:23:44.340 --> 00:23:46.713
We have higher levels of chronic illness,

00:23:47.610 --> 00:23:51.270
vaccine coverage has lapsed,
and so that just makes us

00:23:51.270 --> 00:23:52.103
(device pinging)
more vulnerable

00:23:52.103 --> 00:23:53.190
to infectious diseases in general.

00:23:53.190 --> 00:23:55.860
That's why you're hearing
about measles and polio

00:23:55.860 --> 00:23:58.230
and (chuckles) things that
we didn't have to talk about

00:23:58.230 --> 00:24:01.593
20 years ago quite as much again.

00:24:04.440 --> 00:24:05.273
- Thank you.

00:24:09.510 --> 00:24:13.443
Our next speaker is Dr. Wilmot James.

00:24:15.090 --> 00:24:16.890
There are a lot of endearing
qualities about him

00:24:16.890 --> 00:24:18.330
and one being, that I just noticed,

00:24:18.330 --> 00:24:21.420
that he has a stopwatch in his phone.

00:24:21.420 --> 00:24:23.160
I think that's certainly the academic side

00:24:23.160 --> 00:24:24.420
of his personality,

00:24:24.420 --> 00:24:26.190
not the politician side of his personality

00:24:26.190 --> 00:24:29.962
because politicians don't
know when to stop speaking.

00:24:29.962 --> 00:24:32.160
(audience chuckling)
It's only the professors

00:24:32.160 --> 00:24:35.220
who are timed by other
classes lining up outside.

00:24:35.220 --> 00:24:37.040
So, thanks.

00:24:37.040 --> 00:24:42.040
- Thank you.

00:24:43.103 --> 00:24:45.990
Thank you very much indeed, to Dr. Saad

00:24:45.990 --> 00:24:49.290
and for the invitation
from Yale University

00:24:49.290 --> 00:24:50.580
to speak here today.

00:24:50.580 --> 00:24:51.723
To tell you the truth,

00:24:52.800 --> 00:24:55.170
when I was in the South African
Parliament, you're given

00:24:55.170 --> 00:24:56.603
(phone ringing)
five minutes to speak.

00:24:57.450 --> 00:25:00.270
Normally, that means that
they will shut it down

00:25:00.270 --> 00:25:01.380
after five minutes.

00:25:01.380 --> 00:25:03.506
It's something called the guillotine,

00:25:03.506 --> 00:25:04.616
(audience chuckling)
which means

00:25:04.616 --> 00:25:07.663
you work on 100 words per
minute and you practice it.

00:25:07.663 --> 00:25:08.813
(audience chuckling)
So,

00:25:09.810 --> 00:25:10.950
with that being said,
(claps hands)

00:25:10.950 --> 00:25:13.660
let me speak about

00:25:14.640 --> 00:25:16.200
the subject of vaccines.

00:25:16.200 --> 00:25:19.353
So, I can get the next
slide, my disclaimer slide.

00:25:20.340 --> 00:25:22.680
No conflicts of interest and just thanks

00:25:22.680 --> 00:25:25.560
to all of those individuals
for being of assistance.

00:25:25.560 --> 00:25:26.853
The next slide, please.

00:25:27.720 --> 00:25:30.510
So, colleagues, there's a
growing consensus in the world

00:25:30.510 --> 00:25:33.360
that we need to do three things.

00:25:33.360 --> 00:25:35.880
We need to focus on three areas of work.

00:25:35.880 --> 00:25:40.800
The first has to do with
training and educating

00:25:40.800 --> 00:25:45.510
a new generation of
individuals in public health

00:25:45.510 --> 00:25:48.930
and clinical medicine with
high levels of expertise

00:25:48.930 --> 00:25:52.170
in a distributed way,
globally and to network.

00:25:52.170 --> 00:25:54.627
The second is to upscale surveillance.

00:25:54.627 --> 00:25:55.800
And we speak a lot about
(device chirping)

00:25:55.800 --> 00:25:56.790
real-time surveillance.

00:25:56.790 --> 00:25:58.590
We actually don't have

00:25:58.590 --> 00:26:00.810
global systems of real-time surveillance.

00:26:00.810 --> 00:26:03.330
This is a 10-year long project.

00:26:03.330 --> 00:26:04.350
Not easy to do.

00:26:04.350 --> 00:26:07.320
The third is to have
a decentralized system

00:26:07.320 --> 00:26:10.350
of vaccine manufacturing in the world.

00:26:10.350 --> 00:26:11.853
So, I'm gonna focus on that.

00:26:13.530 --> 00:26:18.530
As you can see on the screen,
there is now a commitment

00:26:19.230 --> 00:26:22.080
to developing vaccines within 100 days,

00:26:22.080 --> 00:26:23.313
which is breathtaking.

00:26:24.330 --> 00:26:27.630
The innovation during
COVID-19 was breathtaking

00:26:27.630 --> 00:26:29.950
in terms of the speed
of vaccine production

00:26:31.320 --> 00:26:34.140
and using new technology platforms.

00:26:34.140 --> 00:26:37.710
After our experience now, we
need to capture lessons learned

00:26:37.710 --> 00:26:41.820
from this period of vaccine development.

00:26:41.820 --> 00:26:43.740
We need to deliver
options for the expansion

00:26:43.740 --> 00:26:45.630
of manufacturing capacity

00:26:45.630 --> 00:26:47.910
in low and middle-income countries

00:26:47.910 --> 00:26:51.120
and we need to establish a
delivery and accountability plan.

00:26:51.120 --> 00:26:52.920
Those are the three elements.

00:26:52.920 --> 00:26:55.020
And I'd like just to share
with you some thoughts

00:26:55.020 --> 00:26:56.910
about what's happening in Africa.

00:26:56.910 --> 00:26:58.143
Next slide, please.

00:27:00.960 --> 00:27:04.770
The framework is being
set largely through CEPI

00:27:04.770 --> 00:27:08.910
and its plan to work
towards shortening the time

00:27:08.910 --> 00:27:11.227
that it takes to produce
vaccines to 100 days.

00:27:11.227 --> 00:27:14.460
It really is quite
incredible what is happening

00:27:14.460 --> 00:27:18.210
in the world of modern
science today during COVID-19.

00:27:18.210 --> 00:27:20.070
This is an effort to even improve it

00:27:20.070 --> 00:27:25.070
and they think it's possible
with the right strategic focus,

00:27:25.410 --> 00:27:28.260
timing, funding and so on.

00:27:28.260 --> 00:27:29.523
Next slide, please.

00:27:30.840 --> 00:27:33.030
Let me just say that,
and you would know this,

00:27:33.030 --> 00:27:36.390
so I would just repeat
this, vaccines are among

00:27:36.390 --> 00:27:38.940
the greatest achievements
of modern health science.

00:27:40.350 --> 00:27:43.140
It's one of the most effective
public health interventions

00:27:43.140 --> 00:27:44.730
available.

00:27:44.730 --> 00:27:47.430
To note, for those of you who follow this,

00:27:47.430 --> 00:27:49.680
vaccination, immunization
rates are falling

00:27:49.680 --> 00:27:50.823
in many countries.

00:27:52.560 --> 00:27:55.440
1 In 5 children in Africa
still do not receive

00:27:55.440 --> 00:27:58.200
all of the necessary and basic vaccines

00:27:58.200 --> 00:28:01.590
and as of 2015, Africa accounted for 33%

00:28:01.590 --> 00:28:05.220
of the world cases of
vaccine preventable diseases

00:28:05.220 --> 00:28:06.270
in children under 5.

00:28:06.270 --> 00:28:11.250
You can see the chart on the
right hand side indicating

00:28:11.250 --> 00:28:16.250
what that 33% amounts to cumulatively.

00:28:16.590 --> 00:28:17.823
Next slide, please.

00:28:19.200 --> 00:28:21.480
Just to note that governments
in Sub-Saharan Africa

00:28:21.480 --> 00:28:25.710
spend, on average, $73
per capita on healthcare.

00:28:25.710 --> 00:28:28.380
That is less than 2% of
the per capita spending

00:28:28.380 --> 00:28:30.240
by high-income governments

00:28:30.240 --> 00:28:33.420
and well below their national needs.

00:28:33.420 --> 00:28:35.970
The figure on the right hand side

00:28:35.970 --> 00:28:37.800
provided by the World Bank.

00:28:37.800 --> 00:28:39.930
I'm sorry, it's not the best
slide, but the bottom one,

00:28:39.930 --> 00:28:44.730
you can see what the contrast
is between the investment made

00:28:44.730 --> 00:28:46.740
by government in Sub-Saharan Africa

00:28:46.740 --> 00:28:48.633
compared to high-income countries.

00:28:49.590 --> 00:28:50.550
That's per capita.

00:28:50.550 --> 00:28:52.203
That is not a crude figure.

00:28:53.340 --> 00:28:56.280
So, you can see where
the slack lies presently.

00:28:56.280 --> 00:28:57.393
Next slide, please.

00:28:59.760 --> 00:29:01.950
It is estimated that every $1 spent

00:29:01.950 --> 00:29:04.140
on childhood immunizations in Africa

00:29:04.140 --> 00:29:06.873
returns $44 in economic benefits,

00:29:07.800 --> 00:29:10.410
just looking at the
economic benefit side of it.

00:29:10.410 --> 00:29:13.560
And the four major vaccine
preventable diseases

00:29:13.560 --> 00:29:16.773
collectively cost the African
region $13 billion each year.

00:29:17.790 --> 00:29:19.110
So, there is the case.

00:29:19.110 --> 00:29:21.723
It saves lives, it extends lives,

00:29:23.100 --> 00:29:26.640
it saves money in terms of
the impact in the longer term.

00:29:26.640 --> 00:29:30.660
It is a completely worthwhile
intervention and investment

00:29:30.660 --> 00:29:33.390
from a moral point of view,
from a lifesaving point of view

00:29:33.390 --> 00:29:35.820
and from an economic point of view.

00:29:35.820 --> 00:29:38.700
At the same time, Africa
produces less than 1%

00:29:38.700 --> 00:29:40.830
of its vaccine needs.

00:29:40.830 --> 00:29:42.420
So, there's the case to be made.

00:29:42.420 --> 00:29:45.000
It's a case to be
presented to politicians.

00:29:45.000 --> 00:29:46.053
Next slide, please.

00:29:48.540 --> 00:29:50.130
Just demographically,

00:29:50.130 --> 00:29:52.500
2030, Africa will have
a working population

00:29:52.500 --> 00:29:53.643
of 1 billion people.

00:29:54.840 --> 00:29:57.570
And secondly, really
important point to emphasize

00:29:57.570 --> 00:30:01.890
in my estimation, you've
got six months in fact.

00:30:01.890 --> 00:30:03.970
It's a small window of opportunity

00:30:04.860 --> 00:30:07.080
to introduce
whole-of-government-initiatives

00:30:07.080 --> 00:30:10.650
to support the establishment
of an integrated comparative

00:30:10.650 --> 00:30:13.470
and cohesive vaccine manufacturing market

00:30:13.470 --> 00:30:14.823
for the African continent.

00:30:15.667 --> 00:30:19.590
COVID-19 was tragic,
but COVID-19 illustrated

00:30:19.590 --> 00:30:22.710
the value of investing in preparedness,

00:30:22.710 --> 00:30:26.583
including in manufacturing of vaccines.

00:30:27.660 --> 00:30:30.577
The picture on the right
hand side just tells you

00:30:30.577 --> 00:30:33.270
what the benefits are in fact.

00:30:33.270 --> 00:30:34.443
Next slide, please.

00:30:38.580 --> 00:30:42.990
This particular illustration
tells you what's going on

00:30:42.990 --> 00:30:47.463
in Africa presently, in terms
of vaccine-related activity.

00:30:48.750 --> 00:30:51.010
The data on the slide is three weeks old

00:30:52.050 --> 00:30:53.730
and we update it regularly.

00:30:53.730 --> 00:30:55.500
But this is a fairly current picture

00:30:55.500 --> 00:30:58.590
and there's one update I'd
like to share with you.

00:30:58.590 --> 00:31:01.390
To note that the WHO has introduced

00:31:02.723 --> 00:31:06.270
a mRNA vaccine transfer technology hub.

00:31:06.270 --> 00:31:09.240
It's based in South Africa,
in the City of Cape Town

00:31:09.240 --> 00:31:13.823
and where I am from, and
has identified seven spokes

00:31:14.850 --> 00:31:17.310
as sites for vaccine manufacturing.

00:31:17.310 --> 00:31:19.860
That is hugely ambitious.

00:31:19.860 --> 00:31:22.050
We believe that South
Africa and Senegal presently

00:31:22.050 --> 00:31:25.893
have the greatest potential
and are focusing on that.

00:31:28.590 --> 00:31:32.400
The countries involved as
targeted entities presently

00:31:32.400 --> 00:31:36.270
are Kenya, Rwanda, Senegal,
South Africa, and Ghana

00:31:36.270 --> 00:31:37.203
as the sites.

00:31:38.490 --> 00:31:39.540
The second category,

00:31:39.540 --> 00:31:41.883
are all the other industrial partnerships.

00:31:42.840 --> 00:31:44.490
Let me just say that presently,

00:31:44.490 --> 00:31:47.490
there is no end-to-end
vaccine manufacturing

00:31:47.490 --> 00:31:50.283
for human applications in
Africa at all presently.

00:31:51.330 --> 00:31:53.133
What we have is fill-and-finish.

00:31:54.390 --> 00:31:58.890
There is a history of end-to-end
vaccine manufacturing,

00:31:58.890 --> 00:32:02.430
starting off with RND and
ending up with a product

00:32:02.430 --> 00:32:06.330
for animals, but even the
underinvestment in that

00:32:06.330 --> 00:32:09.390
has led to a degradation of that system,

00:32:09.390 --> 00:32:11.910
largely in South Africa, in
a place called Onderstepoort,

00:32:11.910 --> 00:32:13.890
which is outside of Pretoria.

00:32:13.890 --> 00:32:15.240
When it comes to plants,

00:32:15.240 --> 00:32:17.340
that's another story for another occasion.

00:32:18.180 --> 00:32:20.850
So, what has emerged, are
other industrial partnerships

00:32:20.850 --> 00:32:24.810
using non-mRNA platforms.

00:32:24.810 --> 00:32:27.510
You can see what the
list looks like there,

00:32:27.510 --> 00:32:30.330
Algeria, Morocco, South
Africa, Egypt, Nigeria,

00:32:30.330 --> 00:32:31.690
Botswana, Uganda

00:32:33.030 --> 00:32:37.740
and what the list is of
developments underway.

00:32:37.740 --> 00:32:40.890
An update, is that Aspen PharmaCare,

00:32:40.890 --> 00:32:43.780
which is the biggest
pharmaceutical company

00:32:44.670 --> 00:32:47.550
on the African continent,
which had a license

00:32:47.550 --> 00:32:51.690
to fill-and-finish J&amp;J, has signed a deal

00:32:51.690 --> 00:32:54.360
with the Serum Institute of India

00:32:54.360 --> 00:32:56.670
to produce four lines of vaccines.

00:32:56.670 --> 00:32:58.830
We don't know what those lines are.

00:32:58.830 --> 00:32:59.663
We will discover.

00:32:59.663 --> 00:33:04.593
So, what you see is a
pharmaceutical company partnering up

00:33:04.593 --> 00:33:07.080
with the Serum Institute of India,

00:33:07.080 --> 00:33:10.140
which of course sees Africa as its market

00:33:10.140 --> 00:33:13.980
and will cultivate that,
or by way is saying,

00:33:13.980 --> 00:33:15.570
that here the private sector is leading,

00:33:15.570 --> 00:33:18.270
It's not government that's
leading on the initiatives.

00:33:19.410 --> 00:33:22.320
I just wanted to point
out, you see down the line,

00:33:22.320 --> 00:33:25.353
Botswana, somebody called
Patrick Soon-Shiong.

00:33:26.616 --> 00:33:30.450
Patrick Soon-Shiong is a
South African physician.

00:33:30.450 --> 00:33:32.070
Has been in Los Angeles.

00:33:32.070 --> 00:33:35.223
Made an enormous amount
of money on an IP issue

00:33:35.223 --> 00:33:37.110
when it came to cancer

00:33:37.110 --> 00:33:39.150
and is now one of the
wealthiest people in the U.S.

00:33:39.150 --> 00:33:43.800
He owns the LA Times, and
he has decided to invest

00:33:43.800 --> 00:33:46.410
in vaccine manufacturing,
both in Botswana,

00:33:46.410 --> 00:33:48.720
as well as South Africa.

00:33:48.720 --> 00:33:53.370
The Botswana operation is
running together with Peter Hotez

00:33:53.370 --> 00:33:55.020
and the Baylor College of Medicine

00:33:55.020 --> 00:33:59.883
and uses traditional means
of producing vaccines.

00:34:01.980 --> 00:34:03.273
The next slide, please.

00:34:04.320 --> 00:34:05.460
This is the amount of money

00:34:05.460 --> 00:34:07.440
that's flowing into Africa presently.

00:34:07.440 --> 00:34:12.240
It's 1.5 billion euros with
300 million euros to be raised.

00:34:12.240 --> 00:34:16.020
It's a lot of money, but on
the grander scheme of things,

00:34:16.020 --> 00:34:17.163
is not that much.

00:34:18.240 --> 00:34:20.220
To note that the money is philanthropic,

00:34:20.220 --> 00:34:25.220
the money is bilateral, and
the money is also multilateral

00:34:25.980 --> 00:34:29.193
and that Africa itself is
contributing very little to that.

00:34:30.330 --> 00:34:33.150
To make this sustainable, will
require greater commitment

00:34:33.150 --> 00:34:36.687
on the part of African
governments to, in fact, invest

00:34:36.687 --> 00:34:38.760
in vaccine manufacturing.

00:34:38.760 --> 00:34:40.293
The major challenges,

00:34:42.030 --> 00:34:46.710
people are sort of mesmerized by mRNA.

00:34:46.710 --> 00:34:51.710
There's no way we can use mRNA
platforms to produce vaccines

00:34:52.170 --> 00:34:53.190
in Africa as a whole.

00:34:53.190 --> 00:34:56.520
It has to be a combination,
a smart combination.

00:34:56.520 --> 00:34:58.710
It focuses on Afri-specific diseases,

00:34:58.710 --> 00:35:01.740
both emerging ones and existing ones.

00:35:01.740 --> 00:35:03.960
To be sustainable, you need to do both.

00:35:03.960 --> 00:35:07.680
You need to produce for an African market

00:35:07.680 --> 00:35:11.460
for routine immunization, as
well as pandemic scale-up,

00:35:11.460 --> 00:35:14.040
scale-down dynamic response.

00:35:14.040 --> 00:35:15.660
Getting it done as a business

00:35:15.660 --> 00:35:19.170
requires a whole range of interventions

00:35:19.170 --> 00:35:23.801
that would make the durancy
of it possible and so on.

00:35:23.801 --> 00:35:25.890
So, all what we're saying,
this is not straightforward.

00:35:25.890 --> 00:35:27.063
Next slide, please.

00:35:29.700 --> 00:35:31.680
So, to meet the public health challenge,

00:35:31.680 --> 00:35:33.570
countries have organized

00:35:33.570 --> 00:35:38.570
on a national, regional and
continental level in Africa.

00:35:39.120 --> 00:35:39.953
They emerged

00:35:39.953 --> 00:35:42.810
the Partnership for African
Vaccine Manufacturing,

00:35:42.810 --> 00:35:44.910
which is an initiative of the AU

00:35:44.910 --> 00:35:47.160
and at the multilateral and global level,

00:35:47.160 --> 00:35:50.340
WHO vaccine technology transfer hubs

00:35:50.340 --> 00:35:53.430
involve the WHO and its partners.

00:35:53.430 --> 00:35:54.663
The next slide, please,

00:35:57.090 --> 00:36:00.540
We set up something called
VacTask in response to a request

00:36:00.540 --> 00:36:04.020
by the South African
Government for a team of us

00:36:04.020 --> 00:36:09.020
to help them get in the business
of manufacturing vaccines.

00:36:11.220 --> 00:36:13.320
So, we set up VacTask, it's called.

00:36:13.320 --> 00:36:17.100
It's an independent advisory
body to governments.

00:36:17.100 --> 00:36:18.910
It's working as a front runner

00:36:20.100 --> 00:36:23.610
for the Partnership for
African Vaccine Manufacturing.

00:36:23.610 --> 00:36:25.770
It's supporting an effort to harmonize

00:36:25.770 --> 00:36:27.000
the regulatory environment.

00:36:27.000 --> 00:36:29.850
This is both product
regulation and price regulation

00:36:29.850 --> 00:36:33.450
and financial regulation,
but in this case,

00:36:33.450 --> 00:36:37.352
product regulation by way
the African Medicines Agency.

00:36:37.352 --> 00:36:38.970
There're 54 countries in Africa.

00:36:38.970 --> 00:36:43.970
If you wanna make vaccines
and need approval of all 55,

00:36:43.980 --> 00:36:44.880
it's a nightmare, right?

00:36:44.880 --> 00:36:48.360
So, there's an effort
to have an AMA do that.

00:36:48.360 --> 00:36:50.100
This is not gonna be straightforward,

00:36:50.100 --> 00:36:52.350
but we need that facility.

00:36:52.350 --> 00:36:55.170
The purpose of VacTask, just
finally, is to accelerate

00:36:55.170 --> 00:36:58.233
and expand Africa's R&amp;D
manufacturing drives,

00:36:59.310 --> 00:37:00.543
checking on my time,

00:37:03.630 --> 00:37:04.890
and therapies.

00:37:04.890 --> 00:37:06.123
Next slide, please.

00:37:07.800 --> 00:37:08.820
This is who we are.

00:37:08.820 --> 00:37:10.950
It's chaired by Jeremy
Farrar at Wellcome Trust.

00:37:10.950 --> 00:37:11.970
I co-chair it.

00:37:11.970 --> 00:37:15.840
You can see it's a very
distinguished list of individuals

00:37:15.840 --> 00:37:17.790
being part of VacTask.

00:37:17.790 --> 00:37:19.143
Next slide, please.

00:37:20.970 --> 00:37:22.440
What we've done to date is,

00:37:22.440 --> 00:37:25.713
we conducted a first fact-finding
mission to South Africa.

00:37:27.210 --> 00:37:30.870
We're currently finalizing a report,

00:37:30.870 --> 00:37:33.690
a set of policy recommendations

00:37:33.690 --> 00:37:36.480
to be presented to the
South African Government.

00:37:36.480 --> 00:37:38.070
We've also hosted virtual meetings

00:37:38.070 --> 00:37:40.440
on the latest developments
in pandemic response.

00:37:40.440 --> 00:37:41.613
The next slide, please,

00:37:43.710 --> 00:37:47.520
We are concerned and make
recommendations about four things.

00:37:47.520 --> 00:37:50.400
First to get procurement right.

00:37:50.400 --> 00:37:52.980
There needs to be harmony
in procurement internally.

00:37:52.980 --> 00:37:55.650
You can't have the
Health Department procure

00:37:55.650 --> 00:37:57.420
on the basis of price

00:37:57.420 --> 00:38:00.930
and you don't talk to Trade and Industry.

00:38:00.930 --> 00:38:04.260
So, there needs to be
internal coordination

00:38:04.260 --> 00:38:07.410
and then there has to be
continental coordination.

00:38:07.410 --> 00:38:10.380
Secondly, the need for
human capital investment

00:38:10.380 --> 00:38:13.110
in manufacturing skills.

00:38:13.110 --> 00:38:15.090
That is not the science part,

00:38:15.090 --> 00:38:18.870
which is in reasonable
shape in South Africa,

00:38:18.870 --> 00:38:21.870
but in good manufacturing practice.

00:38:21.870 --> 00:38:23.280
Those skills are absent.

00:38:23.280 --> 00:38:25.500
You deal with them by importing
those skills immediately,

00:38:25.500 --> 00:38:29.523
but you need to train a
pipeline of people in that area.

00:38:30.510 --> 00:38:32.910
Third point I already
made about the regulation.

00:38:32.910 --> 00:38:35.310
The regulatory environment
needs to be upgraded

00:38:35.310 --> 00:38:36.840
on a major scale,

00:38:36.840 --> 00:38:41.040
and there has to be also R&amp;D
infrastructure investment

00:38:41.040 --> 00:38:46.040
and in adjacent industries
along the value chain

00:38:46.500 --> 00:38:48.270
as we move through.

00:38:48.270 --> 00:38:50.400
The slide on the right hand side,

00:38:50.400 --> 00:38:54.180
is just to illustrate and
also to amplify the fact

00:38:54.180 --> 00:38:56.610
that the biggest
investment that we can make

00:38:56.610 --> 00:38:58.650
is in human skill.

00:38:58.650 --> 00:39:00.524
That's the biggest one.

00:39:00.524 --> 00:39:02.250
And that takes a while,

00:39:02.250 --> 00:39:05.190
but that's something that's
an important challenge.

00:39:05.190 --> 00:39:06.453
The next slide, please.

00:39:07.350 --> 00:39:10.080
We can do all of that,
but if people, (chuckles),

00:39:10.080 --> 00:39:12.300
if people don't trust vaccines,

00:39:12.300 --> 00:39:15.240
and if they don't believe
that is a good thing to have,

00:39:15.240 --> 00:39:17.160
it's a kind of pointless exercise.

00:39:17.160 --> 00:39:20.040
Right now, Africa's sitting
with a surplus of vaccines.

00:39:20.040 --> 00:39:21.660
They don't know what to do with it.

00:39:21.660 --> 00:39:25.260
So, what's important is to have
a model for vaccine take-up

00:39:25.260 --> 00:39:26.523
and just very quickly.

00:39:27.360 --> 00:39:29.430
This is the environment
in which you're operating.

00:39:29.430 --> 00:39:32.250
You can see that when it
comes to trust in science,

00:39:32.250 --> 00:39:34.237
Sub-Saharan Africa,

00:39:34.237 --> 00:39:36.540
(object thudding)
is the lowest

00:39:36.540 --> 00:39:37.533
and is declining.

00:39:39.420 --> 00:39:40.740
The next slide, please.

00:39:40.740 --> 00:39:43.110
When you look at trust in scientists,

00:39:43.110 --> 00:39:44.553
you can see the same thing.

00:39:46.170 --> 00:39:49.890
So, also very low and declining.

00:39:49.890 --> 00:39:51.153
The next slide, please.

00:39:52.980 --> 00:39:57.330
Believe that sources base
coronavirus-related decisions

00:39:57.330 --> 00:39:59.340
on scientific advice by region.

00:39:59.340 --> 00:40:02.820
You can see the level of
distrust in the world.

00:40:02.820 --> 00:40:05.343
Also, Sub-Saharan Africa is quite low,

00:40:07.890 --> 00:40:11.340
but the lowest is Russia and
the Caucuses in Central Asia.

00:40:11.340 --> 00:40:12.543
Next slide, please.

00:40:13.770 --> 00:40:15.180
These are the categories of people

00:40:15.180 --> 00:40:17.340
that are the most trusted in the world,

00:40:17.340 --> 00:40:19.050
starting off with doctors and nurses

00:40:19.050 --> 00:40:22.350
and ending at the bottom,
religious leaders.

00:40:22.350 --> 00:40:23.553
The next slide, please.

00:40:26.040 --> 00:40:29.400
Okay, so that's the context
in which we are operating.

00:40:29.400 --> 00:40:32.700
So, we started a project where
we wanted to interview people

00:40:32.700 --> 00:40:36.240
who, at the late stage,
come for vaccinations

00:40:36.240 --> 00:40:41.240
to understand what the barriers were.

00:40:42.420 --> 00:40:45.060
This is unique in a sense, this study,

00:40:45.060 --> 00:40:48.027
because these are late
stage wait-and-see people

00:40:48.027 --> 00:40:51.810
who come for vaccination
and once you interview them.

00:40:51.810 --> 00:40:54.030
We are running the project
in South Africa and Zimbabwe.

00:40:54.030 --> 00:40:55.023
The next slide.

00:40:59.700 --> 00:41:01.260
This is a publication we made.

00:41:01.260 --> 00:41:02.583
The next slide, please.

00:41:03.750 --> 00:41:07.380
This is to the next slide,
we just wanted to assess

00:41:07.380 --> 00:41:11.610
what the attitudes are
to vaccine mandates.

00:41:11.610 --> 00:41:12.813
The next slide, please.

00:41:14.550 --> 00:41:15.423
The key thing there is

00:41:15.423 --> 00:41:18.240
that there was a high
percentage of individuals

00:41:18.240 --> 00:41:21.180
who supported the use
of vaccines in Zimbabwe.

00:41:21.180 --> 00:41:24.030
You had the normal pattern

00:41:24.030 --> 00:41:27.720
where high-income people
had the least support

00:41:27.720 --> 00:41:29.763
and low-income people the most support.

00:41:30.600 --> 00:41:33.840
The reason has very little to
do with education, or income,

00:41:33.840 --> 00:41:38.840
but to how many women in
particular fall into the category

00:41:39.090 --> 00:41:43.203
of those who had the most
support to our vaccine mandates.

00:41:44.910 --> 00:41:48.390
The important thing was,
is a discovery we found,

00:41:48.390 --> 00:41:53.380
if you can show the next
slide, please, is that

00:41:56.970 --> 00:42:00.870
the individuals who were key to the answer

00:42:00.870 --> 00:42:05.870
around vaccine acceptance,
were mothers of children,

00:42:06.360 --> 00:42:08.280
even in deep rural Zimbabwe.

00:42:08.280 --> 00:42:11.230
It's said they had the most
questions about vaccine safety.

00:42:13.507 --> 00:42:14.340
"Who tested it?

00:42:14.340 --> 00:42:15.957
"Where was it approved?" and so on.

00:42:15.957 --> 00:42:18.450
And clearly you can
understand it's kinda obvious

00:42:18.450 --> 00:42:21.720
that that would be the case.

00:42:21.720 --> 00:42:24.573
So, just to say around
drivers of vaccination,

00:42:25.440 --> 00:42:28.770
the trusted sources of information
were Ministry of Health.

00:42:28.770 --> 00:42:31.170
Your main information methods for them

00:42:31.170 --> 00:42:32.910
were traditional media.

00:42:32.910 --> 00:42:36.360
And the most important
influences had to do

00:42:36.360 --> 00:42:39.630
with who it was that
loved them, basically.

00:42:39.630 --> 00:42:42.130
Those were the ones who
made the real difference

00:42:44.220 --> 00:42:47.400
in terms of accepting vaccines.

00:42:47.400 --> 00:42:48.243
Next slide.

00:42:50.474 --> 00:42:52.560
I've 10 seconds left, I think.

00:42:52.560 --> 00:42:54.760
These were the main
barriers to vaccination.

00:42:55.980 --> 00:42:59.160
As you can see that more
women had major concerns

00:42:59.160 --> 00:43:00.933
about the side effects than men.

00:43:02.790 --> 00:43:05.090
The relationship between
involved in education

00:43:05.940 --> 00:43:08.100
and also internet use.

00:43:08.100 --> 00:43:09.660
You can see what the story is there.

00:43:09.660 --> 00:43:14.070
But let me just conclude by
saying, with the next slide,

00:43:14.070 --> 00:43:15.903
that in the case of South Africa,

00:43:17.370 --> 00:43:20.500
what we found is an
age grading in terms of

00:43:21.450 --> 00:43:26.450
what the impact was of both media

00:43:27.120 --> 00:43:31.410
and what the impact was of
the different influences.

00:43:31.410 --> 00:43:35.400
And you can see that between 18 and 34,

00:43:35.400 --> 00:43:37.410
what the differences were.

00:43:37.410 --> 00:43:40.950
All by way of saying
that, the final slide,

00:43:40.950 --> 00:43:44.500
that what the world needs
to do is develop model

00:43:45.420 --> 00:43:50.420
for successful vaccine
uptake, modulated by region,

00:43:50.430 --> 00:43:53.250
by country and by culture to introduce

00:43:53.250 --> 00:43:56.280
as part of pandemic
preparedness for the next time

00:43:56.280 --> 00:43:59.520
because we actually did
not know what we were doing

00:43:59.520 --> 00:44:01.773
in terms of driving our vaccination rates.

00:44:03.660 --> 00:44:05.160
And so, that's my concluding note,

00:44:05.160 --> 00:44:06.330
is that none of this matters,

00:44:06.330 --> 00:44:09.840
unless there are evidence-based
models for vaccine take-up

00:44:09.840 --> 00:44:13.830
built into the planning
DNA for the next pandemic.

00:44:13.830 --> 00:44:16.302
And with that, thank you very much.

00:44:16.302 --> 00:44:19.996
