Awesome.

00:45:56.280 --> 00:45:58.470
Well, I'm going to be
speaking a little bit

00:45:58.470 --> 00:46:00.570
about the architecture that we need.

00:46:00.570 --> 00:46:03.000
And we've heard from both Wilmot and Jen

00:46:03.000 --> 00:46:05.850
about some of the tools that
we need at the national level,

00:46:05.850 --> 00:46:08.190
at the regional level,
and the global level.

00:46:08.190 --> 00:46:10.680
I'm gonna talk with, I'm
gonna try, to get us up

00:46:10.680 --> 00:46:13.770
to the level of talking about
the whole architecture we need

00:46:13.770 --> 00:46:16.680
for global health security
and pandemic preparedness.

00:46:16.680 --> 00:46:19.110
I'm gonna give you my
bottom line up-front,

00:46:19.110 --> 00:46:22.860
on the first slide, which
is we need to be faster,

00:46:22.860 --> 00:46:25.920
we need to have systems and we
need to practice every time.

00:46:25.920 --> 00:46:26.910
Jen alluded to this,

00:46:26.910 --> 00:46:29.220
when she talked about the firehouse model.

00:46:29.220 --> 00:46:31.380
Nobody, when we think about a hurricane

00:46:31.380 --> 00:46:34.470
and hurricane preparedness,
nobody looks at a community

00:46:34.470 --> 00:46:37.410
that is narrowly escaping
a category for a hurricane

00:46:37.410 --> 00:46:40.267
and asks, "Why did we
have so many sandbags? or,

00:46:40.267 --> 00:46:41.700
"Why did we evacuate?"

00:46:41.700 --> 00:46:44.220
In fact, everyone's grateful
that nothing happens.

00:46:44.220 --> 00:46:46.800
We need to get in the
mentality of overacting

00:46:46.800 --> 00:46:49.320
and that means we have to
have the systems ready to go,

00:46:49.320 --> 00:46:52.413
and we have to know exactly
what we're doing every time.

00:46:53.670 --> 00:46:54.620
Next slide, please.

00:46:56.899 --> 00:46:58.440
I just wanted to start
with the consequences.

00:46:58.440 --> 00:46:59.850
These are the consequences.

00:46:59.850 --> 00:47:01.500
And I think it's pretty
obvious to all of us

00:47:01.500 --> 00:47:04.530
who've been experiencing the
pandemic, but this slide,

00:47:04.530 --> 00:47:07.680
three years ago when Jen,
Wilmot and I were talking about

00:47:07.680 --> 00:47:10.290
our last project together
before the pandemic started,

00:47:10.290 --> 00:47:13.650
this was a pretty unimaginable slide.

00:47:13.650 --> 00:47:14.483
Next slide.

00:47:16.320 --> 00:47:18.150
These are really the consequences.

00:47:18.150 --> 00:47:19.260
People lost their lives.

00:47:19.260 --> 00:47:21.150
The economy lost trillions.

00:47:21.150 --> 00:47:23.940
Proliferation of
disinformation and mistrust.

00:47:23.940 --> 00:47:25.560
Government struggled.

00:47:25.560 --> 00:47:26.393
Next slide.

00:47:29.100 --> 00:47:31.950
Public health and other
governmental officials

00:47:31.950 --> 00:47:34.440
and communities hesitated,

00:47:34.440 --> 00:47:39.060
but there were also a lot of
incentives against acting fast.

00:47:39.060 --> 00:47:42.630
There were disincentives for acting fast.

00:47:42.630 --> 00:47:44.880
What I mean by that, is
that the national level,

00:47:44.880 --> 00:47:48.030
when you don't have a system
that everybody recognizes

00:47:48.030 --> 00:47:50.130
as the way that you're
gonna spring into action

00:47:50.130 --> 00:47:52.418
to respond to a public health emergency

00:47:52.418 --> 00:47:53.550
that are a international concern,

00:47:53.550 --> 00:47:55.290
you're making a decision on your own,

00:47:55.290 --> 00:47:57.390
for your own community,
for your own government,

00:47:57.390 --> 00:47:59.430
even for your own region.

00:47:59.430 --> 00:48:01.860
And there are big consequences
to getting it wrong

00:48:01.860 --> 00:48:03.810
if you don't have trust

00:48:03.810 --> 00:48:05.940
that the way that you're
going to respond and act

00:48:05.940 --> 00:48:07.440
is a certain way every time.

00:48:07.440 --> 00:48:08.273
Next slide.

00:48:09.510 --> 00:48:11.160
I'm gonna really start by just defining

00:48:11.160 --> 00:48:13.410
what I mean by global health security

00:48:13.410 --> 00:48:14.850
because it's a term we use a lot.

00:48:14.850 --> 00:48:16.380
We also use pandemic preparedness.

00:48:16.380 --> 00:48:18.480
We use health system strengthening.

00:48:18.480 --> 00:48:20.310
Global health security, in my mind,

00:48:20.310 --> 00:48:24.300
is being prepared to
prevent, detect and respond

00:48:24.300 --> 00:48:26.760
to public health emergencies
that have high consequences,

00:48:26.760 --> 00:48:28.200
including public health emergencies

00:48:28.200 --> 00:48:29.760
of international concern,

00:48:29.760 --> 00:48:33.000
whether they're naturally
occurring, accidentally caused,

00:48:33.000 --> 00:48:34.200
or deliberately caused.

00:48:34.200 --> 00:48:36.630
So, in all of those cases,
that's health security.

00:48:36.630 --> 00:48:38.490
It's a piece of the health system,

00:48:38.490 --> 00:48:39.900
but it's not the only piece.

00:48:39.900 --> 00:48:40.733
Next slide.

00:48:42.390 --> 00:48:45.840
I think it's important to
actually look at the nesting.

00:48:45.840 --> 00:48:48.030
It's hard to tell with a red here,

00:48:48.030 --> 00:48:50.070
but these are interlocking circles.

00:48:50.070 --> 00:48:52.890
I see health security and
that broader health system

00:48:52.890 --> 00:48:54.750
as not in conflict with one another.

00:48:54.750 --> 00:48:56.940
But sometimes those terms
are defined that way,

00:48:56.940 --> 00:49:01.170
so I wanted to just state my
view on this topic up front,

00:49:01.170 --> 00:49:03.870
that preparing for pandemics is a piece

00:49:03.870 --> 00:49:05.670
of being prepared for an outbreak

00:49:05.670 --> 00:49:08.550
that might happen on a
national, or a regional basis,

00:49:08.550 --> 00:49:10.740
is a piece of our broader health system.

00:49:10.740 --> 00:49:12.990
So doctors, nurses, and access to care,

00:49:12.990 --> 00:49:17.040
which is absolutely vital
for pandemic preparedness,

00:49:17.040 --> 00:49:21.120
and it's all a piece of our
broader global health system.

00:49:21.120 --> 00:49:24.300
So, all of this, in order to
be prepared for a pandemic,

00:49:24.300 --> 00:49:28.020
we need to have systems that
work at all of these levels.

00:49:28.020 --> 00:49:28.853
Next slide.

00:49:31.440 --> 00:49:34.020
This is a slide that
I drew from a document

00:49:34.020 --> 00:49:35.190
that Jen also referenced,

00:49:35.190 --> 00:49:36.960
which is the Global Health Security Index,

00:49:36.960 --> 00:49:38.580
a project that all three of us have worked

00:49:38.580 --> 00:49:40.140
on over the last couple of years.

00:49:40.140 --> 00:49:43.200
This is a map that just shows
preparedness by country.

00:49:43.200 --> 00:49:45.780
This was put together
by the Economist Impact,

00:49:45.780 --> 00:49:47.160
by the Nuclear Threat Initiative,

00:49:47.160 --> 00:49:48.990
by the Johns Hopkins
Center for Health Security

00:49:48.990 --> 00:49:52.200
and now the Brown University
is part of it too.

00:49:52.200 --> 00:49:56.550
It looked at over 100 questions,
all WHO member states,

00:49:56.550 --> 00:49:59.280
and it asked what capacities
are there in place

00:49:59.280 --> 00:50:02.970
to be prepared, to prevent,
to detect and respond?

00:50:02.970 --> 00:50:04.770
But it also looked at other capacities.

00:50:04.770 --> 00:50:06.870
So, it looked at things
that were particular

00:50:06.870 --> 00:50:09.450
to the ability of a country to
be prepared for an outbreak,

00:50:09.450 --> 00:50:12.570
or a public health emergency,
like your laboratory system,

00:50:12.570 --> 00:50:13.830
your public health workforce,

00:50:13.830 --> 00:50:16.140
your ability to vaccinate your people.

00:50:16.140 --> 00:50:19.260
It also looked at things like
numbers of doctors and nurses.

00:50:19.260 --> 00:50:21.210
It looked at trust in government.

00:50:21.210 --> 00:50:24.090
It looked at trust in
science in the last version,

00:50:24.090 --> 00:50:25.530
and it looked at a country's ability

00:50:25.530 --> 00:50:28.470
to deal with crises in general.

00:50:28.470 --> 00:50:29.303
Next slide.

00:50:30.720 --> 00:50:34.080
The bottom line is the
average score taken together

00:50:34.080 --> 00:50:38.100
across all those countries,
was 38.9 out of 100.

00:50:38.100 --> 00:50:40.710
That's just what countries could do.

00:50:40.710 --> 00:50:41.730
That doesn't necessarily mean

00:50:41.730 --> 00:50:43.560
that they even used those capacities.

00:50:43.560 --> 00:50:45.390
This tool was not
intended to be predictive

00:50:45.390 --> 00:50:46.740
of how countries could do.

00:50:46.740 --> 00:50:48.120
Simply was meant to look at

00:50:48.120 --> 00:50:51.450
what countries would be able
to do if they actually mustered

00:50:51.450 --> 00:50:53.550
the capacities that they had.

00:50:53.550 --> 00:50:54.383
Next slide.

00:50:57.480 --> 00:50:59.310
I wanna talk about architecture

00:50:59.310 --> 00:51:01.710
and what existed before COVID-19

00:51:01.710 --> 00:51:03.840
in order to fill those preparedness gaps

00:51:03.840 --> 00:51:07.140
and be prepared to respond in the face

00:51:07.140 --> 00:51:10.500
of an emerging infectious
disease threat like COVID-19.

00:51:10.500 --> 00:51:15.500
Drawing back to SARS, to
the SARS epidemic in 2003,

00:51:15.870 --> 00:51:17.460
the international health regulations,

00:51:17.460 --> 00:51:19.140
which is essentially the agreement

00:51:19.140 --> 00:51:21.360
that World Health Organization
member states have

00:51:21.360 --> 00:51:24.660
with one another saying that
they all plan to be prepared

00:51:24.660 --> 00:51:27.210
for a public health emergency
of international concern,

00:51:27.210 --> 00:51:28.530
were updated.

00:51:28.530 --> 00:51:31.560
They were updated because
the world recognized,

00:51:31.560 --> 00:51:36.030
back in 2003 when SARS hit,
that countries were not prepared

00:51:36.030 --> 00:51:38.670
to deal with a public health emergency.

00:51:38.670 --> 00:51:41.100
The world gave a deadline of 2012

00:51:41.100 --> 00:51:43.950
in order for all countries to be prepared.

00:51:43.950 --> 00:51:45.900
In that intervening time, what happened

00:51:45.900 --> 00:51:49.080
because the IHRs were good, but imperfect

00:51:49.080 --> 00:51:50.760
in that they didn't define

00:51:50.760 --> 00:51:54.480
exactly what countries needed
to do in order to be prepared.

00:51:54.480 --> 00:51:56.280
And when countries hit the 2012 mark,

00:51:56.280 --> 00:51:57.540
they either didn't know,

00:51:57.540 --> 00:52:00.240
or they didn't know about each
other's level of preparedness

00:52:00.240 --> 00:52:02.580
because there was no
publication, no record,

00:52:02.580 --> 00:52:04.410
of how prepared countries actually were.

00:52:04.410 --> 00:52:08.430
There was no red, yellow, green
sort of stoplight mechanism.

00:52:08.430 --> 00:52:12.570
So, following 2005 and then
into 2012, when we recognized

00:52:12.570 --> 00:52:15.090
that most countries weren't
actually going to hit

00:52:15.090 --> 00:52:17.700
the deadline that was put into place,

00:52:17.700 --> 00:52:20.040
the Ebola epidemic hit in 2014.

00:52:20.040 --> 00:52:24.120
There was a lot of work
already going on post-2012,

00:52:24.120 --> 00:52:26.100
this deadline that everyone missed.

00:52:26.100 --> 00:52:29.670
And countries decided they
were going to put in place

00:52:29.670 --> 00:52:31.680
a scoring scheme to actually tell

00:52:31.680 --> 00:52:34.290
whether or not countries
were prepared themselves

00:52:34.290 --> 00:52:37.170
and to be able to send people
to each other's borders

00:52:37.170 --> 00:52:39.846
to actually look at
how prepared they were.

00:52:39.846 --> 00:52:43.230
The WHO Joint External Evaluation
Tool was put into place

00:52:43.230 --> 00:52:44.063
to do that.

00:52:44.063 --> 00:52:45.600
You can actually go online and look at

00:52:45.600 --> 00:52:48.450
how prepared countries
are at the WHO website.

00:52:48.450 --> 00:52:51.000
We used a lot of these
questions and added to those

00:52:51.000 --> 00:52:53.190
as the basis for the Global
Health Security Index,

00:52:53.190 --> 00:52:55.290
which you can also go online
and look at preparedness

00:52:55.290 --> 00:52:56.610
for every country.

00:52:56.610 --> 00:53:00.120
Around the same time, many
governments, over 60 governments,

00:53:00.120 --> 00:53:02.700
launched something called the
Global Health Security Agenda,

00:53:02.700 --> 00:53:04.837
which is a coalition of
countries that decided,

00:53:04.837 --> 00:53:06.697
"We should meet regularly to talk about

00:53:06.697 --> 00:53:09.420
"how prepared countries
are for pandemics."

00:53:09.420 --> 00:53:12.210
During this period of time,
this rough period of time

00:53:12.210 --> 00:53:15.990
during which H1N1 influenza
happened, Ebola happened,

00:53:15.990 --> 00:53:17.973
SARS happened, Zika happened.

00:53:18.874 --> 00:53:22.013
Countries were worried
about influenza cases, H7N9

00:53:22.013 --> 00:53:23.610
and H5N1, which were high consequence,

00:53:23.610 --> 00:53:26.583
but weren't spreading as
an epidemic, or a pandemic.

00:53:27.420 --> 00:53:28.740
A lot happened.

00:53:28.740 --> 00:53:31.920
Budgets actually did
increase for health security

00:53:31.920 --> 00:53:32.850
in some spaces,

00:53:32.850 --> 00:53:35.280
but not across our whole public
health preparedness space.

00:53:35.280 --> 00:53:38.160
Looking at Jen's slide,
totally agree with her.

00:53:38.160 --> 00:53:41.730
Nonetheless, when you look
at that massive level of gap,

00:53:41.730 --> 00:53:46.710
38.9 out of 100, when you
look at the huge swath of work

00:53:46.710 --> 00:53:49.350
to be done on things like,
how do we make vaccines,

00:53:49.350 --> 00:53:51.570
how do we deliver them,
how do we distribute them,

00:53:51.570 --> 00:53:53.910
how do we deal with vaccine update,

00:53:53.910 --> 00:53:56.790
funding and political will
was wholly insufficient

00:53:56.790 --> 00:53:57.623
to be prepared.

00:53:57.623 --> 00:54:00.510
And we didn't have sort
of recognized systems

00:54:00.510 --> 00:54:03.330
for how we would spring
into action, or triggers

00:54:03.330 --> 00:54:05.160
for those systems to work

00:54:05.160 --> 00:54:08.700
when an emerging infectious
disease threat like COVID-19,

00:54:08.700 --> 00:54:10.260
came to call.

00:54:10.260 --> 00:54:11.093
Next slide.

00:54:12.690 --> 00:54:16.290
So, what resulted is that
a lot had to be created

00:54:16.290 --> 00:54:19.350
from scratch, on the fly, in 2020.

00:54:19.350 --> 00:54:21.270
This is an example of a lot of great work

00:54:21.270 --> 00:54:22.740
that happened during the pandemic,

00:54:22.740 --> 00:54:25.920
but it wasn't in existence beforehand.

00:54:25.920 --> 00:54:29.160
So, as one key example,
the ACT Accelerator,

00:54:29.160 --> 00:54:32.160
the Access to COVID-19 Tools Accelerator,

00:54:32.160 --> 00:54:35.790
was created in 2020 by governments,

00:54:35.790 --> 00:54:37.800
by non-governmental organizations,

00:54:37.800 --> 00:54:42.030
by international organizations
like the WHO, like UNICEF,

00:54:42.030 --> 00:54:45.060
Wellcome Trust, the Gates
Foundation, multiple governments,

00:54:45.060 --> 00:54:48.330
the Global Fund against
HIV Aids, TB and Malaria,

00:54:48.330 --> 00:54:51.720
Gavi, the Vaccine Alliance,
came together and said,

00:54:51.720 --> 00:54:56.700
how are we going to get tools
like supplies, vaccines,

00:54:56.700 --> 00:55:00.300
tests, therapeutics, personal
protective equipment, oxygen

00:55:00.300 --> 00:55:02.520
to countries that don't
have access to these things?

00:55:02.520 --> 00:55:05.280
So, that was created on
the fly during the pandemic

00:55:05.280 --> 00:55:07.770
and it's a great example
of what can be done.

00:55:07.770 --> 00:55:11.100
There's also a lot cropping
up about how it could,

00:55:11.100 --> 00:55:14.580
or maybe could be improved,
for the next pandemic.

00:55:14.580 --> 00:55:16.230
But this is a big open question.

00:55:16.230 --> 00:55:17.760
How will it be maintained?

00:55:17.760 --> 00:55:19.950
Which pieces of it will be maintained?

00:55:19.950 --> 00:55:22.380
What is the best way to
keep this work going?

00:55:22.380 --> 00:55:24.453
And how do we fund that work?

00:55:25.530 --> 00:55:28.650
Regions like the African
Union, and Wilmot talked a lot

00:55:28.650 --> 00:55:30.600
about some of the work
to manufacture vaccines,

00:55:30.600 --> 00:55:33.630
there's also an African
Union platform called AVAT

00:55:33.630 --> 00:55:36.570
that came online in order
to distribute vaccines

00:55:36.570 --> 00:55:37.800
on the African continent.

00:55:37.800 --> 00:55:40.770
So, regions working with
the global architecture,

00:55:40.770 --> 00:55:41.880
it was bumpy.

00:55:41.880 --> 00:55:44.310
There wasn't always agreement
on how that would work,

00:55:44.310 --> 00:55:46.827
but these structures are in existence now

00:55:46.827 --> 00:55:49.110
and we need to figure out as a world,

00:55:49.110 --> 00:55:51.450
how best to maintain
and move them forward.

00:55:51.450 --> 00:55:55.350
COVAX came into play, which
is the vaccine platform piece

00:55:55.350 --> 00:55:57.381
of the Act Accelerator.

00:55:57.381 --> 00:56:01.500
The G7, the world's
largest economies, the G20,

00:56:01.500 --> 00:56:04.320
they all raised a lot of
money, billions of dollars,

00:56:04.320 --> 00:56:07.770
in order to be able to
fill some of these gaps.

00:56:07.770 --> 00:56:12.599
Finally, this year, the
world agreed, the WTO agreed

00:56:12.599 --> 00:56:13.432
(device pinging)
to waive

00:56:13.432 --> 00:56:17.133
intellectual property on
COVID-19-related vaccines.

00:56:18.420 --> 00:56:21.780
All of this, there were
Presidential Summit

00:56:21.780 --> 00:56:24.360
that the U.S. Government and
other spurred into action.

00:56:24.360 --> 00:56:28.710
So, a lot of work happened,
but still we landed in a place

00:56:28.710 --> 00:56:30.960
where millions of people died

00:56:30.960 --> 00:56:32.940
and we didn't have the
systems that we needed.

00:56:32.940 --> 00:56:35.850
So, this is the architecture as it exists

00:56:35.850 --> 00:56:37.110
and what happens next,

00:56:37.110 --> 00:56:41.880
next slide, is really a big open question.

00:56:41.880 --> 00:56:43.950
So, I'm gonna talk for
just a couple more minutes

00:56:43.950 --> 00:56:46.140
about where some of this might go

00:56:46.140 --> 00:56:47.790
and what recommendations there are.

00:56:47.790 --> 00:56:48.960
Some things that have been done,

00:56:48.960 --> 00:56:50.370
some things that have not yet been done,

00:56:50.370 --> 00:56:52.620
but I wanna make it
really clear to everyone

00:56:52.620 --> 00:56:55.950
that this is an open question
that still needs to be shaped

00:56:55.950 --> 00:56:57.270
with agreement around the world

00:56:57.270 --> 00:56:59.040
and it's being actively
discussed this week

00:56:59.040 --> 00:57:00.810
at the UN General Assembly.

00:57:00.810 --> 00:57:01.643
Next slide.

00:57:04.230 --> 00:57:07.140
So, I highly recommend
if you haven't looked

00:57:07.140 --> 00:57:10.260
at some of the reports that
have come out, I do think

00:57:10.260 --> 00:57:13.200
that the international
experts that were commissioned

00:57:13.200 --> 00:57:15.000
to do a number of reports,
that I've mention,

00:57:15.000 --> 00:57:17.190
did a great job doing them
and there's some really great

00:57:17.190 --> 00:57:18.150
executive summaries
(audience member coughs)

00:57:18.150 --> 00:57:20.220
that I think really put in...

00:57:20.220 --> 00:57:21.960
A lot of converging recommendations

00:57:21.960 --> 00:57:23.853
came out of this pandemic so far.

00:57:24.840 --> 00:57:27.000
The G20 High-Level Independent Panel

00:57:27.000 --> 00:57:29.310
looked primarily at financing.

00:57:29.310 --> 00:57:32.670
Do we have enough money to
prepare for the next pandemic?

00:57:32.670 --> 00:57:33.960
Do we have enough money

00:57:33.960 --> 00:57:36.000
to be able to respond
to the next pandemic?

00:57:36.000 --> 00:57:37.560
And where is it going to come from?

00:57:37.560 --> 00:57:39.810
What's the role of
multilateral development banks

00:57:39.810 --> 00:57:41.100
like the World Bank.

00:57:41.100 --> 00:57:45.540
And also, do we need a new
fund for pandemic preparedness?

00:57:45.540 --> 00:57:48.030
That is one recommendation
that was acted on,

00:57:48.030 --> 00:57:49.050
I'm really happy to say.

00:57:49.050 --> 00:57:51.960
Just a couple of weeks ago,
the World Bank did launch

00:57:51.960 --> 00:57:56.130
a new fund for pandemic
preparedness with over $1.4 billion.

00:57:56.130 --> 00:57:57.180
It's not enough.

00:57:57.180 --> 00:57:59.850
The vision is to have at
least $10 billion a year

00:57:59.850 --> 00:58:03.180
for this fund, but it
is a big step forward

00:58:03.180 --> 00:58:04.770
and it is a recommendation that was shared

00:58:04.770 --> 00:58:06.870
by a number of international experts.

00:58:06.870 --> 00:58:08.310
And it's something that
the U.S. Government

00:58:08.310 --> 00:58:11.013
and other partners around
the world really pushed for.

00:58:12.060 --> 00:58:14.160
At the same time, the Independent Panel

00:58:14.160 --> 00:58:17.400
for Pandemic Preparedness
and Response, the IPPPR,

00:58:17.400 --> 00:58:19.290
under the leadership of
Ellen Johnson Sirleaf,

00:58:19.290 --> 00:58:21.300
the Former President of Liberia

00:58:21.300 --> 00:58:24.930
and Helen Clark, the Former
Prime Minister of New Zealand,

00:58:24.930 --> 00:58:27.570
put out a report that
focused a lot on leadership

00:58:27.570 --> 00:58:30.780
and governance for the
next pandemic threat.

00:58:30.780 --> 00:58:33.210
They recommended a Global
Health Threats Council,

00:58:33.210 --> 00:58:35.670
a set of world leaders
that would meet regularly

00:58:35.670 --> 00:58:38.130
on pandemic threats, should come together

00:58:38.130 --> 00:58:41.040
and that should have
happened in the last year.

00:58:41.040 --> 00:58:44.160
That recommendation has
not yet been acted upon.

00:58:44.160 --> 00:58:45.570
So, it's a piece of the architecture

00:58:45.570 --> 00:58:48.540
that is still out there in question.

00:58:48.540 --> 00:58:50.700
Wilmot alluded to the 100 Days Mission.

00:58:50.700 --> 00:58:53.460
This is in effect, a recommendation

00:58:53.460 --> 00:58:56.370
that came out of the United
Kingdom's G7 presidency

00:58:56.370 --> 00:59:00.570
last year, to be able, on
a very systematized basis,

00:59:00.570 --> 00:59:03.930
to look at all emerging,
potentially pandemic pathogens

00:59:03.930 --> 00:59:06.480
and develop platforms
for vaccine candidates,

00:59:06.480 --> 00:59:10.800
so that we can basically be
ready to do clinical trials

00:59:10.800 --> 00:59:11.633
and then scale

00:59:11.633 --> 00:59:12.660
and produce vaccines
(siren wailing)

00:59:12.660 --> 00:59:15.480
for almost any emerging
potential pathogens.

00:59:15.480 --> 00:59:19.230
This is a really great playbook,
but it is not yet funded

00:59:19.230 --> 00:59:20.850
and there isn't yet a roadmap

00:59:20.850 --> 00:59:23.310
for how we're actually going
to then produce those vaccines.

00:59:23.310 --> 00:59:25.440
I think Wilmot did a
great job of laying out

00:59:25.440 --> 00:59:27.240
some of the challenges there,

00:59:27.240 --> 00:59:28.890
The Lancet Commission published

00:59:28.890 --> 00:59:31.830
a large set of recommendations
just last week,

00:59:31.830 --> 00:59:33.391
some of which converge

00:59:33.391 --> 00:59:34.224
(siren wailing)
with these other documents

00:59:34.224 --> 00:59:36.150
and some of which are also new.

00:59:36.150 --> 00:59:38.490
So, there's a lot of
work that's been done.

00:59:38.490 --> 00:59:40.170
Not all of it's been acted on.

00:59:40.170 --> 00:59:41.003
Next slide.

00:59:44.100 --> 00:59:46.050
There's also a question
about what we're going to do

00:59:46.050 --> 00:59:50.160
about accidental and
deliberate biological events.

00:59:50.160 --> 00:59:51.780
All of the technologies that we need

00:59:51.780 --> 00:59:54.990
to be able to be prepared
for the next pandemic threat

00:59:54.990 --> 00:59:57.960
are absolutely vital,
but also, we run the risk

00:59:57.960 --> 01:00:00.720
of inadvertently creating the catastrophe

01:00:00.720 --> 01:00:03.840
that we're trying to prevent
if that technology is misused,

01:00:03.840 --> 01:00:05.760
accidentally, or on purpose.

01:00:05.760 --> 01:00:08.580
So, there's a movement to
launch a new organization called

01:00:08.580 --> 01:00:11.070
the International Biosecurity
and Biosafety Initiative

01:00:11.070 --> 01:00:14.430
for Science to bring
together funders of research,

01:00:14.430 --> 01:00:16.410
the private sector, and governments

01:00:16.410 --> 01:00:17.820
in order to tackle this issue.

01:00:17.820 --> 01:00:20.283
And the Nuclear Threat Initiative
is launching this effort

01:00:20.283 --> 01:00:21.210
this fall.

01:00:21.210 --> 01:00:23.250
That's another piece of the architecture

01:00:23.250 --> 01:00:27.180
that has not yet been formed,
but is starting to be built.

01:00:27.180 --> 01:00:28.013
Next slide.

01:00:31.590 --> 01:00:33.600
When we look at the diagram on the right,

01:00:33.600 --> 01:00:35.910
these are my go-to pieces

01:00:35.910 --> 01:00:37.920
for the global health
security architecture,

01:00:37.920 --> 01:00:40.380
the pandemic preparedness
architecture that we need.

01:00:40.380 --> 01:00:42.990
When we look at financing,
we've made some strides

01:00:42.990 --> 01:00:45.450
at preparedness financing with
the launching of the new fund

01:00:45.450 --> 01:00:46.710
a couple of weeks ago.

01:00:46.710 --> 01:00:48.990
There's not yet agreement
though, on things like

01:00:48.990 --> 01:00:51.750
how do we get surge financing into place,

01:00:51.750 --> 01:00:55.350
so that low income countries
can get in line to buy vaccines

01:00:55.350 --> 01:00:56.880
early on in the next pandemic.

01:00:56.880 --> 01:00:59.820
That's a big open question
that still needs to be resolved

01:00:59.820 --> 01:01:02.010
in order to make the
manufacturing architecture,

01:01:02.010 --> 01:01:04.710
that Wilmot outlined, actually work.

01:01:04.710 --> 01:01:08.760
Country capacity, huge number
of gaps in country capacity,

01:01:08.760 --> 01:01:11.460
but increasing political will and interest

01:01:11.460 --> 01:01:15.090
and probably a window with
COVID-19 and now monkeypox,

01:01:15.090 --> 01:01:17.940
to maintain interest at the
highest levels of government

01:01:17.940 --> 01:01:20.430
in actually filling those gaps.

01:01:20.430 --> 01:01:23.010
That's critical that these two things

01:01:23.010 --> 01:01:24.120
are related to one another.

01:01:24.120 --> 01:01:26.760
That donors, that this
new fund in that countries

01:01:26.760 --> 01:01:29.370
look at their massive number
of gaps in many cases,

01:01:29.370 --> 01:01:30.900
including in our own country

01:01:30.900 --> 01:01:33.330
and prioritize which gaps to fill

01:01:33.330 --> 01:01:35.730
and how that will actually
make them more prepared.

01:01:35.730 --> 01:01:38.310
So, there's technical work,
but also political work

01:01:38.310 --> 01:01:40.230
that needs to be done here.

01:01:40.230 --> 01:01:42.870
On access to medical
countermeasures in a crisis,

01:01:42.870 --> 01:01:45.720
a lot happened that's
really COVID-19 specific

01:01:45.720 --> 01:01:47.850
with the ACT Accelerator being created,

01:01:47.850 --> 01:01:49.230
with COVAX being created,

01:01:49.230 --> 01:01:52.680
with regional organizations
like AVAT being created,

01:01:52.680 --> 01:01:55.860
but none of that has been
agreed on, or concretized

01:01:55.860 --> 01:01:57.690
for other emerging pandemic threats.

01:01:57.690 --> 01:01:59.463
That's another big open question.

01:02:00.300 --> 01:02:02.640
On political will, there
has not been agreement

01:02:02.640 --> 01:02:04.530
to create a new structure,

01:02:04.530 --> 01:02:06.180
like a global health threats council.

01:02:06.180 --> 01:02:08.460
In the absence of that, here are questions

01:02:08.460 --> 01:02:11.370
about who will ring the alarm
bell at the highest levels

01:02:11.370 --> 01:02:14.223
in the next 2020 scenario that we face.

01:02:15.240 --> 01:02:17.040
And then finally, accountability.

01:02:17.040 --> 01:02:18.330
So, there has been a movement,

01:02:18.330 --> 01:02:21.060
one that the U.S. Government
has supported, among others,

01:02:21.060 --> 01:02:23.100
to amend the International
Health Regulations,

01:02:23.100 --> 01:02:24.360
to make them sharper,

01:02:24.360 --> 01:02:27.240
to make the reporting
requirements more specific.

01:02:27.240 --> 01:02:29.640
And there's also an active
negotiation in Geneva

01:02:29.640 --> 01:02:32.670
around the need for a pandemic
instrument, or treaty,

01:02:32.670 --> 01:02:37.670
to bring some of this
together and sort of glue it.

01:02:37.740 --> 01:02:38.573
Next slide.

01:02:40.740 --> 01:02:42.003
So, I'm just gonna leave you with this.

01:02:42.003 --> 01:02:43.920
What this is supposed to represent,

01:02:43.920 --> 01:02:47.250
is that we have all the
ingredients that we need.

01:02:47.250 --> 01:02:50.550
Maybe the only good thing that's
come out of this pandemic,

01:02:50.550 --> 01:02:52.470
is we now have all the tools.

01:02:52.470 --> 01:02:54.870
We have the ingredients, we
have the thought leadership

01:02:54.870 --> 01:02:57.040
that we need in order to make the systems

01:02:58.325 --> 01:03:01.080
that will make us prepared for
anything that comes our way.

01:03:01.080 --> 01:03:03.870
But now we really need the
recipes and the practice

01:03:03.870 --> 01:03:06.510
so that the next time we
actually are prepared,

01:03:06.510 --> 01:03:09.630
and we can actually bake whatever
it is that you wanna bake,

01:03:09.630 --> 01:03:10.463
that would taste
(Beth chuckles)

01:03:10.463 --> 01:03:13.080
the best to you and we need
to be prepared to do it

01:03:13.080 --> 01:03:16.830
every single time, using
the tools that we now have.

01:03:16.830 --> 01:03:17.663
I'll stop there.

01:03:18.560 --> 01:03:21.560
(audience applauds)

01:03:23.444 --> 01:03:27.361
- Any clarifying questions
from Beth initially?

01:03:28.200 --> 01:03:30.090
Are you stretching or raising your hand?

01:03:30.090 --> 01:03:31.569
- Oh, sorry.
(audience member sniggers)

01:03:31.569 --> 01:03:32.402
- Okay. That's fine.

01:03:32.402 --> 01:03:33.630
Just checking.

01:03:33.630 --> 01:03:35.730
All right, so we can go to the panel now.

01:03:35.730 --> 01:03:37.080
So, we'll come as promised,

01:03:37.080 --> 01:03:40.473
we'll come to Dr. Stan Bowman's question.

01:03:42.035 --> 01:03:43.950
And that will be your question.

01:03:43.950 --> 01:03:45.690
If you remember, you can--
- I do.

01:03:45.690 --> 01:03:50.040
- Start, or we can ask
Dr. Bowman to rephrase.

01:03:50.040 --> 01:03:52.290
- First of all, to say,
that's a great question.

01:03:52.290 --> 01:03:56.850
So, you know, vaccines
are all about volume.

01:03:56.850 --> 01:04:01.850
And the African market is
one market and most companies

01:04:02.250 --> 01:04:04.950
would have to look at the bigger picture

01:04:04.950 --> 01:04:06.870
in terms of providing it.

01:04:06.870 --> 01:04:07.703
That's the first point.

01:04:07.703 --> 01:04:11.130
The second is that vaccines
things are made by industry,

01:04:11.130 --> 01:04:12.750
They're made by companies.

01:04:12.750 --> 01:04:15.180
They're not made by
governments and therefore

01:04:15.180 --> 01:04:18.437
there has to be collaboration
with industry on this effort.

01:04:20.010 --> 01:04:22.650
So, governments are (indistinct) enable

01:04:22.650 --> 01:04:26.100
Third is the point I made before.

01:04:26.100 --> 01:04:28.890
It has to be a combination,
a smart combination,

01:04:28.890 --> 01:04:33.890
of routine, immunization,
manufacturing and supply

01:04:36.060 --> 01:04:38.880
together with the ability
to scale up and scale down

01:04:38.880 --> 01:04:39.713
the pandemic.

01:04:40.920 --> 01:04:43.950
I'm making very general
points, but that's required.

01:04:43.950 --> 01:04:46.140
For it to be sustainable
(object clatters)

01:04:46.140 --> 01:04:48.136
beyond the initial investments,

01:04:48.136 --> 01:04:48.969
(zipper scratching)

01:04:48.969 --> 01:04:52.080
African governments would have
to put somebody on the table

01:04:52.080 --> 01:04:54.980
for this issue, and that's
gonna take some political work.

01:04:56.091 --> 01:04:58.530
- Okay.
(faintly speaking)

01:04:58.530 --> 01:05:00.840
- There's really not yet a roadmap

01:05:00.840 --> 01:05:03.510
for when you look at something
like the 100 Days Mission,

01:05:03.510 --> 01:05:07.350
which is incredibly ambitious,
the ability to make a vaccine

01:05:07.350 --> 01:05:10.920
for any potentially pandemic
threat within a 100 days.

01:05:10.920 --> 01:05:14.070
Then, what people colloquially
call, the 200 Day Mission,

01:05:14.070 --> 01:05:16.470
which is then the ability to scale that up

01:05:16.470 --> 01:05:18.540
and then produce it and distribute it.

01:05:18.540 --> 01:05:21.540
When you take that
complexity and then look at

01:05:21.540 --> 01:05:22.980
a continent like Africa

01:05:22.980 --> 01:05:25.860
and a plan for vaccine
manufacturing, and you think

01:05:25.860 --> 01:05:28.410
where should different
platforms be produced,

01:05:28.410 --> 01:05:30.720
how can that couple with
routine immunizations,

01:05:30.720 --> 01:05:33.510
so that vaccines will actually
be purchased and procured,

01:05:33.510 --> 01:05:34.710
it's complex.

01:05:34.710 --> 01:05:36.510
I think it's so complex that some people

01:05:36.510 --> 01:05:38.403
are just sort of frozen.

01:05:39.540 --> 01:05:40.373
What we need to do,

01:05:40.373 --> 01:05:41.760
is actually start putting
roadmaps out there,

01:05:41.760 --> 01:05:43.080
putting ideas on the table

01:05:43.080 --> 01:05:45.898
for how this architecture
can be maintained.

01:05:45.898 --> 01:05:46.731
Do you agree with that?

01:05:46.731 --> 01:05:49.140
- I do agree with that,
but also to say that

01:05:49.140 --> 01:05:51.898
right now, the corporate
sector's leading the effort.

01:05:51.898 --> 01:05:53.250
- Okay.
- On the African continent,

01:05:53.250 --> 01:05:56.370
Aspen PharmaCare is the biggest company.

01:05:56.370 --> 01:05:58.510
So, it has corporate interest and

01:06:00.060 --> 01:06:04.800
there is a need to derisk when it comes to

01:06:04.800 --> 01:06:06.723
sort of local manufacturing sites.

01:06:08.948 --> 01:06:10.350
So, we have to make sure

01:06:10.350 --> 01:06:12.510
that we have a proper private partnership

01:06:12.510 --> 01:06:16.770
with proper agreements around,
beneficiary built into it.

01:06:16.770 --> 01:06:19.320
- So, I think, another thing if I may add,

01:06:19.320 --> 01:06:22.650
and first disclaimer, I
sit on the Gavi Board,

01:06:22.650 --> 01:06:26.100
but this is my personal
opinion, which is that

01:06:26.100 --> 01:06:28.920
one of Gavi's, which was formerly called,

01:06:28.920 --> 01:06:30.870
Gavi, The Vaccine..

01:06:30.870 --> 01:06:32.520
Now called Gavi, The Vaccine Alliance.

01:06:32.520 --> 01:06:35.100
It was called Global Alliance for Vaccines

01:06:35.100 --> 01:06:36.123
and immunizations.

01:06:38.730 --> 01:06:40.200
One of the mandates, one of the reasons

01:06:40.200 --> 01:06:42.450
why countries put money into that entity,

01:06:42.450 --> 01:06:45.360
is its market-shaping role.

01:06:45.360 --> 01:06:49.410
If there ever were any case

01:06:49.410 --> 01:06:54.410
for Gavi flexing its muscle,
is to shape the market

01:06:55.200 --> 01:06:56.760
in Sub-Saharan, actually Africa,

01:06:56.760 --> 01:07:01.760
not just Sub-Saharan Africa,
continent-wide market-shaping

01:07:01.830 --> 01:07:05.340
in favor of sustaining manufacturing.

01:07:05.340 --> 01:07:06.453
Having said that,

01:07:09.012 --> 01:07:13.470
in order for the capacity to survive,

01:07:13.470 --> 01:07:17.130
it will have to be a
market-based solution.

01:07:17.130 --> 01:07:20.340
Some players in that ecosystem,

01:07:20.340 --> 01:07:22.800
that's the very nature of markets.

01:07:22.800 --> 01:07:27.800
We have tried government-led
manufacturing around the world.

01:07:28.350 --> 01:07:29.640
It rarely works.

01:07:29.640 --> 01:07:34.350
There are exceptions, but it rarely works

01:07:34.350 --> 01:07:39.350
truly, wholly nationalized
vaccine manufacturing system.

01:07:39.820 --> 01:07:40.680
(device pinging)
So, on the one hand,

01:07:40.680 --> 01:07:43.380
I think Gavi has a role to play in this.

01:07:43.380 --> 01:07:46.770
On the other hand, it is
through sort of nudging markets,

01:07:46.770 --> 01:07:48.150
shaping markets,

01:07:48.150 --> 01:07:51.873
not encouraging 100%
nationalized manufacturing.

01:07:53.220 --> 01:07:54.480
So, I'll come to...

01:07:54.480 --> 01:07:55.545
Sorry,

01:07:55.545 --> 01:07:56.378
you were gonna say something.
- No, just to say,

01:07:56.378 --> 01:07:57.570
which means we must get procurement right.

01:07:57.570 --> 01:07:58.980
- [Saad] Yeah, absolutely.

01:07:58.980 --> 01:07:59.813
That's market shaping,

01:07:59.813 --> 01:08:01.260
essentially.
- Yeah.

01:08:01.260 --> 01:08:03.550
- So, a couple of questions and I'll have

01:08:04.620 --> 01:08:08.463
two versions of the same
question from both of you,

01:08:08.463 --> 01:08:10.530
from Jennifer and Beth.

01:08:10.530 --> 01:08:12.120
Jennifer, I'll start with you.

01:08:12.120 --> 01:08:16.060
What are two mistakes U.S.
policy-makers made in this

01:08:17.282 --> 01:08:18.900
(object thudding)
pandemic?

01:08:18.900 --> 01:08:20.463
- Just two?

01:08:20.463 --> 01:08:21.743
(all chuckle)
- Just, well, top two.

01:08:22.860 --> 01:08:25.341
- Let me see how I can lump it. (chuckles)

01:08:25.341 --> 01:08:27.480
A big enough bin to catch a lot of stuff.

01:08:27.480 --> 01:08:31.800
I think Beth hit on it in
terms of that acting fast

01:08:31.800 --> 01:08:33.123
and then acting,

01:08:34.813 --> 01:08:35.646
(object crashing)
given the possibility

01:08:35.646 --> 01:08:37.683
of the worst case scenario.

01:08:39.810 --> 01:08:43.140
We saw quite...

01:08:43.140 --> 01:08:44.447
This actually happened in COVID

01:08:44.447 --> 01:08:48.000
and it happened in monkeypox, same thing.

01:08:48.000 --> 01:08:51.270
One was, testing was our critical failure.

01:08:51.270 --> 01:08:54.390
The first one was marred by,
the rollout of testing in COVID

01:08:54.390 --> 01:08:57.570
was marred by technical difficulties.

01:08:57.570 --> 01:08:59.160
But there was a period of time there

01:08:59.160 --> 01:09:00.570
where we didn't act on the urgency.

01:09:00.570 --> 01:09:03.337
We didn't think, "Hmm,
this could get really bad.

01:09:03.337 --> 01:09:07.380
"How can we scale to how badly it can be?"

01:09:07.380 --> 01:09:10.830
Then we saw that play out in
COVID, sorry, in monkeypox

01:09:10.830 --> 01:09:14.100
where we had the tests already rolled out.

01:09:14.100 --> 01:09:16.350
We didn't have to overcome
that technical hurdle

01:09:16.350 --> 01:09:19.230
that was already there,
but that infrastructure

01:09:19.230 --> 01:09:21.150
was really scaled to a much smaller event.

01:09:21.150 --> 01:09:24.300
It wasn't looking ahead
to a more distributed,

01:09:24.300 --> 01:09:27.300
community-wide event whereby
you would need to have

01:09:27.300 --> 01:09:29.040
testing more distributed.

01:09:29.040 --> 01:09:31.650
So, I think that was the
first one, was what Beth said,

01:09:31.650 --> 01:09:34.590
sort of failure to think
ahead, how bad could this be

01:09:34.590 --> 01:09:36.900
and what do we need to start
putting into place now,

01:09:36.900 --> 01:09:39.783
given the possibility
of where this could go?

01:09:40.710 --> 01:09:45.710
I think the other thing
was that we really need to

01:09:46.170 --> 01:09:48.690
take a hard look at our
health system and make sure

01:09:48.690 --> 01:09:52.650
our responses are integrated
into our health system

01:09:52.650 --> 01:09:55.950
because that really is the
first point of contact.

01:09:55.950 --> 01:09:59.840
But also, I think we need to take a look

01:09:59.840 --> 01:10:03.750
at how equipped our health
systems are for these events

01:10:03.750 --> 01:10:07.200
and really perhaps
rethink healthcare models.

01:10:07.200 --> 01:10:11.610
This just-in-time, very lean
organizations just don't work

01:10:11.610 --> 01:10:12.600
when there's an emergency.

01:10:12.600 --> 01:10:14.370
And unfortunately there's
lots of emergencies.

01:10:14.370 --> 01:10:15.453
It's not just COVID.

01:10:16.350 --> 01:10:17.880
I was telling a group of students earlier,

01:10:17.880 --> 01:10:19.440
we did a study just before the pandemic,

01:10:19.440 --> 01:10:21.960
looking at hospitals in New York City

01:10:21.960 --> 01:10:25.560
and how ready they were
for the 2017-flu season,

01:10:25.560 --> 01:10:29.160
which rivaled the 2009-pandemic
in terms of volume.

01:10:29.160 --> 01:10:32.070
They really, really struggled then.

01:10:32.070 --> 01:10:34.050
That was, I think, an omen

01:10:34.050 --> 01:10:36.510
for how difficult things
would be in COVID.

01:10:36.510 --> 01:10:37.740
- Yeah, you're absolutely right.

01:10:37.740 --> 01:10:39.540
So, brilliant capacity of both of you,

01:10:39.540 --> 01:10:41.430
actually, all three of you alluded to.

01:10:41.430 --> 01:10:45.330
After, in emergencies like
recruiting firefighters

01:10:45.330 --> 01:10:47.820
after your house starts burning.

01:10:47.820 --> 01:10:51.840
So, you don't call them at that time.

01:10:51.840 --> 01:10:53.853
So, I'll ask you, Beth,

01:10:55.341 --> 01:10:57.660
what do you think were
the two biggest mistakes

01:10:57.660 --> 01:10:59.583
that global policy-makers made?

01:11:00.750 --> 01:11:04.530
- Yeah, well, I'll just
say in 2020, I think...

01:11:04.530 --> 01:11:08.190
I'll just say that the U.S. Government

01:11:08.190 --> 01:11:11.640
was exiting the World Health
Organization, the global stage,

01:11:11.640 --> 01:11:14.010
around the time that the
world, in my opinion,

01:11:14.010 --> 01:11:16.890
needed U.S. leadership the most.

01:11:16.890 --> 01:11:18.510
So, I think that was a huge mistake

01:11:18.510 --> 01:11:21.120
and I think that dogged the response

01:11:21.120 --> 01:11:25.680
for months and months and
months, and probably still

01:11:25.680 --> 01:11:30.390
makes it harder for us when
we are looking at how best

01:11:30.390 --> 01:11:32.340
to fix some of these
architectural questions

01:11:32.340 --> 01:11:34.050
that I just put up on the screen.

01:11:34.050 --> 01:11:34.890
So, that's number one.

01:11:34.890 --> 01:11:39.810
I think you can be frustrated
with the global system.

01:11:39.810 --> 01:11:43.800
You can be rightly asking for
the global system to do more.

01:11:43.800 --> 01:11:45.847
You can look at the
United Nations and say,

01:11:45.847 --> 01:11:50.610
"I want you to do better,"
but leaving, exiting,

01:11:50.610 --> 01:11:53.460
only hurts people, including
people in your own country.

01:11:53.460 --> 01:11:56.343
So, I think that was a
huge, a huge mistake.

01:11:57.210 --> 01:11:58.170
The second thing I'll say,

01:11:58.170 --> 01:12:01.263
and maybe I'll just bring the
question up to the level of,

01:12:02.723 --> 01:12:05.010
what do we, as policy-makers
in the United States

01:12:05.010 --> 01:12:06.510
and globally, fail to do

01:12:06.510 --> 01:12:08.580
in every emerging
infectious disease crisis

01:12:08.580 --> 01:12:09.870
that I've been a part of?

01:12:09.870 --> 01:12:12.300
And I'll include myself in this.

01:12:12.300 --> 01:12:15.580
I think that we fail to look
at the worst case scenario

01:12:17.340 --> 01:12:18.960
and plan backwards from that.

01:12:18.960 --> 01:12:21.930
That doesn't mean that we
act without any evidence,

01:12:21.930 --> 01:12:25.950
or we put in place things
that are factually incorrect,

01:12:25.950 --> 01:12:27.750
but it does mean that we need tools.

01:12:27.750 --> 01:12:30.240
Back to some of the things
that Jennifer said in her talk.

01:12:30.240 --> 01:12:33.120
We do need tools that are ready

01:12:33.120 --> 01:12:34.950
in the absence of information,

01:12:34.950 --> 01:12:36.570
in the absence of perfect evidence,

01:12:36.570 --> 01:12:39.750
and in the face of the uncertainty
that we will always face

01:12:39.750 --> 01:12:42.834
at the beginning of a
public health crisis.

01:12:42.834 --> 01:12:43.667
So, we need to be prepared

01:12:43.667 --> 01:12:45.690
for the absolute worst
thing that can happen,

01:12:45.690 --> 01:12:47.910
or the multiple worst case scenarios.

01:12:47.910 --> 01:12:50.984
Not just the thing that we
think most likely will happen.

01:12:50.984 --> 01:12:52.020
I'll just say, I think public health,

01:12:52.020 --> 01:12:55.140
in general, public health
officials have so little resources

01:12:55.140 --> 01:12:57.720
they're trained to be able
to and want to respond

01:12:57.720 --> 01:13:00.990
the thing that's most likely
because that's all they can do.

01:13:00.990 --> 01:13:03.150
We have to resource public
health appropriately

01:13:03.150 --> 01:13:06.240
and train the next generation
of public health leaders

01:13:06.240 --> 01:13:09.150
to be thinking about decisions
for the worst case scenarios.

01:13:09.150 --> 01:13:12.510
That time that we lose in
not preparing, sets us back

01:13:12.510 --> 01:13:15.206
and puts us behind the curb every time.

01:13:15.206 --> 01:13:16.039
- I agree.

01:13:16.039 --> 01:13:16.872
I couldn't agree more.

01:13:16.872 --> 01:13:20.160
I think it wasn't
technology that failed us.

01:13:20.160 --> 01:13:25.160
PCR was a technology that
has been with us for decades.

01:13:25.500 --> 01:13:29.670
Testing and masks, masks I'm wearing,

01:13:29.670 --> 01:13:32.375
this technology is pretty straightforward.

01:13:32.375 --> 01:13:34.140
It was designed, actually
even the best version of it,

01:13:34.140 --> 01:13:37.607
was designed in 2009, 2010, cetera.

01:13:37.607 --> 01:13:39.270
It was governance.

01:13:39.270 --> 01:13:43.560
If we don't have the capacity
to make effective decisions,

01:13:43.560 --> 01:13:47.130
I think we will continue to
see these kinds of things.

01:13:47.130 --> 01:13:48.000
So, I'll open up...

01:13:48.000 --> 01:13:49.252
Sorry, you--
- Yes,

01:13:49.252 --> 01:13:50.277
I just wanted to add, one
of the things that I think

01:13:50.277 --> 01:13:51.450
one of the failures is,

01:13:51.450 --> 01:13:53.130
we stumbled on risk communication

01:13:53.130 --> 01:13:53.963
- Hmm.
- Hmm.

01:13:55.906 --> 01:13:57.210
- It's that's partly because--
(Jennifer speaking faintly)

01:13:57.210 --> 01:13:58.740
- Something like that.
- Yeah.

01:13:58.740 --> 01:14:01.443
- Part of it's the difficulty
of a moving target.

01:14:02.653 --> 01:14:04.380
But there were some basic principles

01:14:04.380 --> 01:14:06.840
around risk communication
that were simply not followed

01:14:06.840 --> 01:14:09.720
by many countries in the world instance.

01:14:09.720 --> 01:14:12.520
Inconsistent communication
changed the people speaking

01:14:13.680 --> 01:14:16.975
and we stumbled in many respects.

01:14:16.975 --> 01:14:20.457
So, the public also had to
face this disinformation

01:14:20.457 --> 01:14:21.290
and misinformation.

01:14:21.290 --> 01:14:23.370
We understand that, but
in the middle of that,

01:14:23.370 --> 01:14:25.860
I think strategies for
better risk communication

01:14:25.860 --> 01:14:27.060
they really need to be developed

01:14:27.060 --> 01:14:28.470
for the future.
- Well,

01:14:28.470 --> 01:14:30.150
you're speaking to the
choir 'cause some of us

01:14:30.150 --> 01:14:33.660
were jumping up and down
in February and March.

01:14:33.660 --> 01:14:36.630
We did our, here at Yale, we
did our first national survey

01:14:36.630 --> 01:14:39.780
on February 9th, 2000,

01:14:39.780 --> 01:14:43.260
looking at the behavioral
and communications response,

01:14:43.260 --> 01:14:44.810
looked at what were the sources

01:14:47.162 --> 01:14:49.080
of information that
were trusted, et cetera,

01:14:49.080 --> 01:14:52.170
and what should be the response
before it was named COVID.

01:14:52.170 --> 01:14:56.197
We did our first national survey
around vaccines on May 9th,

01:14:58.262 --> 01:14:59.095
2020.

01:14:59.095 --> 01:15:00.030
So, you're absolutely right.

01:15:00.030 --> 01:15:02.940
And we thought, as a global community,

01:15:02.940 --> 01:15:05.700
that we need to bring the best science,

01:15:05.700 --> 01:15:08.430
and this is mainstream thinking, forward,

01:15:08.430 --> 01:15:10.650
and we need to insist on the rigor

01:15:10.650 --> 01:15:13.320
of vaccine development science,

01:15:13.320 --> 01:15:16.500
but not the rigor on vaccine
communication science.

01:15:16.500 --> 01:15:17.720
And that was...

01:15:18.600 --> 01:15:20.460
Very sensible folks thought

01:15:20.460 --> 01:15:22.650
that it was just furrowing your brow

01:15:22.650 --> 01:15:26.430
and speaking on prime
time TV was sufficient.

01:15:26.430 --> 01:15:31.210
And there were no principles,
no evidence to be utilized

01:15:32.460 --> 01:15:34.020
in terms of how to
effectively communicate.

01:15:34.020 --> 01:15:35.760
So, you are absolutely right.

01:15:35.760 --> 01:15:39.000
And next time we better not do that.

01:15:39.000 --> 01:15:44.000
But monkeypox is not a good sign for that.

01:15:44.400 --> 01:15:49.400
So, I'm not that optimistic
that this will not happen again.

01:15:50.730 --> 01:15:52.590
I think it's not...

01:15:52.590 --> 01:15:57.570
It's up to the next generation
of public health leaders

01:15:57.570 --> 01:15:58.930
and scholars and

01:16:00.627 --> 01:16:02.573
(zipper screeching)
implementers

01:16:02.573 --> 01:16:03.810
and policy developers to make sure

01:16:03.810 --> 01:16:05.460
that this is not an afterthought.

01:16:06.674 --> 01:16:07.507
- I think we also need people

01:16:07.507 --> 01:16:08.340
who are training in public health

01:16:08.340 --> 01:16:10.725
and people who are
training in basic science

01:16:10.725 --> 01:16:11.910
(object crashing drowns out)
to do cross-strains--

01:16:11.910 --> 01:16:12.990
- Yeah.
- even more effectively.

01:16:12.990 --> 01:16:14.206
And what I mean by that,

01:16:14.206 --> 01:16:15.900
is that we were developing
new vaccine platforms

01:16:15.900 --> 01:16:18.540
that had never been used
for mRNA vaccine platforms,

01:16:18.540 --> 01:16:20.700
while you were trying to
communicate to the public

01:16:20.700 --> 01:16:21.690
about how they should trust

01:16:21.690 --> 01:16:22.767
these vaccines.
- Yeah.

01:16:22.767 --> 01:16:25.170
So, the scientific development was amazing

01:16:25.170 --> 01:16:29.400
and revolutionary, but that
set us back even further

01:16:29.400 --> 01:16:31.140
with the trust and science communication

01:16:31.140 --> 01:16:32.820
because we were not
just talking about tools

01:16:32.820 --> 01:16:35.790
people were already nervous
about, some segments of society.

01:16:35.790 --> 01:16:37.260
We were talking about brand new tools

01:16:37.260 --> 01:16:38.790
that hadn't actually been tested before.

01:16:38.790 --> 01:16:41.220
So, I think figuring out how to have

01:16:41.220 --> 01:16:43.230
that kind of communication with the public

01:16:43.230 --> 01:16:45.120
around a new and uncertain science,

01:16:45.120 --> 01:16:46.770
makes it even more complicated.

01:16:46.770 --> 01:16:48.180
- That's absolutely true.

01:16:48.180 --> 01:16:49.840
So, we'll jump to questions

01:16:50.937 --> 01:16:52.890
from participants and audience,

01:16:52.890 --> 01:16:56.970
but we'll go to online
questions first, so, maybe...

01:16:56.970 --> 01:16:57.803
Yes?

01:16:59.220 --> 01:17:00.990
- [Assistant] I have two questions,

01:17:00.990 --> 01:17:03.210
I think this might be for Wilmot,

01:17:03.210 --> 01:17:07.200
related to vaccine manufacturing
in those seven countries

01:17:07.200 --> 01:17:09.273
that were kind of identifiers.

01:17:10.800 --> 01:17:11.730
Why were they chosen?

01:17:11.730 --> 01:17:14.010
So, that's maybe question one.

01:17:14.010 --> 01:17:17.400
The second question, for those countries,

01:17:17.400 --> 01:17:19.680
are there plans for skill-building

01:17:19.680 --> 01:17:22.893
to ensure sufficient
human resources as well?

01:17:25.080 --> 01:17:26.760
- Excellent question.

01:17:26.760 --> 01:17:28.593
The choice of countries,

01:17:30.660 --> 01:17:34.210
when it came to developing the spokes

01:17:35.310 --> 01:17:38.340
for the mRNA transfer technology,

01:17:38.340 --> 01:17:39.940
was a selection made by the WHO.

01:17:41.820 --> 01:17:45.450
That had to do with whether
there was existing partners

01:17:45.450 --> 01:17:48.660
in those countries on various aspects.

01:17:48.660 --> 01:17:51.810
So, Tunisia for example,
is very well-positioned

01:17:51.810 --> 01:17:53.463
on the bioinformatic side,

01:17:54.780 --> 01:17:56.070
but it lacks others and so on.

01:17:56.070 --> 01:17:58.740
So, that was one set of criteria.

01:17:58.740 --> 01:18:01.040
The other had to do with
what companies chose.

01:18:02.670 --> 01:18:07.380
So, companies chose to cite work in Kenya,

01:18:07.380 --> 01:18:08.310
which makes a lot of sense.

01:18:08.310 --> 01:18:10.740
Kenya has a lot of interest and experience

01:18:10.740 --> 01:18:14.850
in developing animal vaccines

01:18:14.850 --> 01:18:17.610
because of the animal health experience.

01:18:17.610 --> 01:18:20.310
South Africa probably
has the longest reach.

01:18:20.310 --> 01:18:23.343
So, part of it's just company choices,

01:18:25.830 --> 01:18:27.813
so it's a combination of those things.

01:18:31.140 --> 01:18:32.970
- [Saad] Any other question from?

01:18:32.970 --> 01:18:34.620
- [Assistant] Sure.

01:18:34.620 --> 01:18:36.330
Second question--
- Can you say the name

01:18:36.330 --> 01:18:37.803
of who's asking?
- Sure.

01:18:39.330 --> 01:18:40.590
Jennifer, this question comes from

01:18:40.590 --> 01:18:42.240
Dr. Christina Towers (speaking faintly)

01:18:42.240 --> 01:18:44.433
who you met earlier today.

01:18:45.420 --> 01:18:49.530
She was appreciative of your
note, when you noted that

01:18:49.530 --> 01:18:53.160
one thing you would invest in
was community health workers.

01:18:53.160 --> 01:18:56.160
Could you expound on
that a little bit more?

01:18:56.160 --> 01:18:59.443
Why and how you feel
that would help prepare

01:19:00.575 --> 01:19:01.408
for the next. (faintly speaking)

01:19:01.408 --> 01:19:04.710
- Yeah, I think community health
workers have the potential

01:19:04.710 --> 01:19:09.710
to touch on many areas that
we saw deficiencies in.

01:19:09.720 --> 01:19:12.090
One is clearly on health
services and reaching people

01:19:12.090 --> 01:19:14.190
and continuing to provide,

01:19:14.190 --> 01:19:18.540
not just care for pandemic-specific care,

01:19:18.540 --> 01:19:21.570
but also all the other
care that has to happen

01:19:21.570 --> 01:19:23.280
during the pandemic that doesn't stop.

01:19:23.280 --> 01:19:28.280
I think we cannot put blinders
on and only focus on a virus

01:19:28.380 --> 01:19:30.720
and not consider the whole
health needs of communities.

01:19:30.720 --> 01:19:33.720
And that's, I think,
a lesson that we learn

01:19:33.720 --> 01:19:34.950
every time there's an emergency.

01:19:34.950 --> 01:19:37.170
So, community health workers are important

01:19:37.170 --> 01:19:39.060
for supplementing the
care and extending care

01:19:39.060 --> 01:19:42.180
and providing care where
community needs are.

01:19:42.180 --> 01:19:43.410
But they also potentially help

01:19:43.410 --> 01:19:45.093
on the risk communication side.

01:19:46.560 --> 01:19:47.910
I think that was clearly something

01:19:47.910 --> 01:19:50.880
that we had a hard time doing.

01:19:50.880 --> 01:19:52.680
We didn't really have plans for

01:19:52.680 --> 01:19:56.880
as Beth and Wilmot and Saad
said, talking about, we were

01:19:56.880 --> 01:19:57.780
great on the technology,
(object clatters)

01:19:57.780 --> 01:19:59.700
but we didn't think about
how we were gonna roll it out

01:19:59.700 --> 01:20:01.470
and get people to wanna take it.

01:20:01.470 --> 01:20:04.140
I will just tell you, anecdotally,
in the last two years,

01:20:04.140 --> 01:20:05.700
I have talked to hundreds
and hundreds of people

01:20:05.700 --> 01:20:07.350
about COVID vaccines.

01:20:07.350 --> 01:20:10.320
And one of the key
commonalities that I have found

01:20:10.320 --> 01:20:13.560
in those conversations when
people are asking me questions

01:20:13.560 --> 01:20:16.380
and they sort of suggest trust,

01:20:16.380 --> 01:20:18.420
not sure who to trust may be an issue,

01:20:18.420 --> 01:20:20.340
they commonly don't have
a healthcare provider

01:20:20.340 --> 01:20:21.360
in their lives.

01:20:21.360 --> 01:20:24.540
So, making sure we have people
who are in people's lives,

01:20:24.540 --> 01:20:27.150
not just, suddenly they show
up in the midst of a pandemic,

01:20:27.150 --> 01:20:28.620
but they're in people's lives day to day,

01:20:28.620 --> 01:20:32.070
dealing with the various
medical issues that could arise

01:20:32.070 --> 01:20:34.020
throughout someone's lifespan,

01:20:34.020 --> 01:20:37.350
then to also be able to broker
conversations about vaccines,

01:20:37.350 --> 01:20:39.297
to be able to connect people
to the services they need

01:20:39.297 --> 01:20:41.370
and to be able to get connected,

01:20:41.370 --> 01:20:44.253
that sort of bottom-up
approach is really important.

01:20:45.120 --> 01:20:46.650
- Yeah, absolutely.

01:20:46.650 --> 01:20:50.189
Even in your work you
talked about, Wilmot,

01:20:50.189 --> 01:20:54.120
that healthcare providers
were the most trusted

01:20:54.120 --> 01:20:55.380
source of vaccine information.

01:20:55.380 --> 01:20:59.040
We've been finding in a lot of those work

01:20:59.040 --> 01:21:01.860
for the last 22 years, consistently shows,

01:21:01.860 --> 01:21:05.430
irrespective of geography,
the top most trusted

01:21:05.430 --> 01:21:08.250
source of vaccine information
is a healthcare provider.

01:21:08.250 --> 01:21:12.750
So that means access to a
healthcare provider, but also...

01:21:12.750 --> 01:21:17.340
Again, one of the most lost
opportunity in this country was,

01:21:17.340 --> 01:21:20.970
and beyond, frankly as well, was a lack of

01:21:20.970 --> 01:21:24.660
a national CME program to
train healthcare providers

01:21:24.660 --> 01:21:27.453
on evidence-based communications methods.

01:21:27.453 --> 01:21:29.280
- Yeah.
- And it exists.

01:21:29.280 --> 01:21:31.290
At Yale, we created a CME.

01:21:31.290 --> 01:21:34.710
It's the most subscribed CME program.

01:21:34.710 --> 01:21:39.710
The second-most was a third of attendance

01:21:40.140 --> 01:21:42.420
where people took it for certification.

01:21:42.420 --> 01:21:46.652
And that was states mandatory CME.

01:21:46.652 --> 01:21:47.940
So, there is a demand out there,

01:21:47.940 --> 01:21:49.770
but you were saying something
else, I guess, as well?

01:21:49.770 --> 01:21:51.780
- No, I was gonna say,
there's enormous variation.

01:21:51.780 --> 01:21:56.780
So, healthcare workers obviously
come first as a category,

01:21:57.000 --> 01:21:59.970
but the most trusted
institution in Tunisia,

01:21:59.970 --> 01:22:00.903
is the military.

01:22:01.900 --> 01:22:03.598
The most trusted institution in the Congo,

01:22:03.598 --> 01:22:05.354
is a Catholic Church.

01:22:05.354 --> 01:22:07.100
And the most trusted
institution in South Africa

01:22:07.100 --> 01:22:08.580
is the President of South Africa.

01:22:08.580 --> 01:22:11.373
It's enormous variation
about how trust works.

01:22:12.210 --> 01:22:13.830
Also, just to quickly answer

01:22:13.830 --> 01:22:16.470
the second part of that question
that I failed to answer,

01:22:16.470 --> 01:22:19.950
is that when it comes to
vaccine manufacturing,

01:22:19.950 --> 01:22:22.620
you're talking about the
ecosystem you build around,

01:22:22.620 --> 01:22:24.390
including relations with universities,

01:22:24.390 --> 01:22:27.000
where most of your scientists are located.

01:22:27.000 --> 01:22:29.210
And then as I pointed
out, where the gap is,

01:22:29.210 --> 01:22:32.100
is training in good manufacturing practice

01:22:32.100 --> 01:22:33.180
in making vaccines.

01:22:33.180 --> 01:22:36.360
This is the pharma side, on
the pharmacological side,

01:22:36.360 --> 01:22:40.830
that's where the gap is and
to what's required is a fund,

01:22:40.830 --> 01:22:43.740
a scholarships fund, to
train people in that area.

01:22:43.740 --> 01:22:45.510
That takes a little
while, so in the meantime,

01:22:45.510 --> 01:22:47.463
there's a need to import skills.

01:22:49.650 --> 01:22:52.323
- Beth, I'll ask the
next question from you.

01:22:54.210 --> 01:22:57.810
When you talk to folks
and public health leaders

01:22:57.810 --> 01:22:59.970
specifically around the world,

01:22:59.970 --> 01:23:01.590
especially in low and
middle-income countries,

01:23:01.590 --> 01:23:05.870
not everyone is on board
with the term biosecurity

01:23:05.870 --> 01:23:09.090
and biopreparedness, and even where

01:23:09.090 --> 01:23:11.673
whatever version of the word security is.

01:23:12.673 --> 01:23:14.550
So, you sometimes get pushback from there.

01:23:14.550 --> 01:23:17.220
Any other thoughts on framing

01:23:17.220 --> 01:23:20.193
our collective preparedness
in those terms?

01:23:21.240 --> 01:23:23.490
- Yeah, I think one thing I'll say is,

01:23:23.490 --> 01:23:25.097
that it depends on who you talk to.

01:23:25.097 --> 01:23:27.660
So, if you're talking to
public health officials,

01:23:27.660 --> 01:23:29.970
sometimes there is concern
with using that term.

01:23:29.970 --> 01:23:31.920
If you're talking to leaders

01:23:31.920 --> 01:23:33.450
in ministries of foreign affairs,

01:23:33.450 --> 01:23:36.540
or in national governments
at the highest levels,

01:23:36.540 --> 01:23:37.770
there's less concern.

01:23:37.770 --> 01:23:40.200
That again, that's a broad generalization,

01:23:40.200 --> 01:23:42.990
and it really depends on
the country that you're in

01:23:42.990 --> 01:23:45.060
and the people that you're talking to.

01:23:45.060 --> 01:23:46.980
So, I always start by defining

01:23:46.980 --> 01:23:50.490
what I mean by health security
because it makes it clear

01:23:50.490 --> 01:23:53.640
that I'm talking, not only about security

01:23:53.640 --> 01:23:56.100
as in national security,
or national defense,

01:23:56.100 --> 01:23:58.620
I'm talking about security for people,

01:23:58.620 --> 01:24:01.413
our public security and our public safety.

01:24:02.490 --> 01:24:04.890
I like the term health security.

01:24:04.890 --> 01:24:06.570
I think that it's an important term.

01:24:06.570 --> 01:24:08.250
I think that we should continue to use it,

01:24:08.250 --> 01:24:09.300
but I think it has to be framed

01:24:09.300 --> 01:24:11.910
in the context of the
broader health system.

01:24:11.910 --> 01:24:14.340
I think that you just
made an outstanding point

01:24:14.340 --> 01:24:16.110
in the conversation
about healthcare workers

01:24:16.110 --> 01:24:17.850
and community health.

01:24:17.850 --> 01:24:19.830
If we don't actually have
a strong healthcare system,

01:24:19.830 --> 01:24:22.470
we know that we're not going
to have global health security.

01:24:22.470 --> 01:24:24.450
So, you have to look at
those two things together.

01:24:24.450 --> 01:24:27.720
But I do think that there is a touchiness

01:24:27.720 --> 01:24:30.300
amongst some in the health community

01:24:30.300 --> 01:24:32.490
to using the security terminology.

01:24:32.490 --> 01:24:34.020
One challenge I find with that,

01:24:34.020 --> 01:24:36.420
wherever you sit on that debate,

01:24:36.420 --> 01:24:40.860
is that when we have a
crisis, like COVID-19,

01:24:40.860 --> 01:24:43.380
pretty quickly things move
from ministries of health

01:24:43.380 --> 01:24:44.400
as spokespeople
(objects clatter)

01:24:44.400 --> 01:24:45.540
up to level of government.

01:24:45.540 --> 01:24:47.490
And that's always going to happen.

01:24:47.490 --> 01:24:48.900
You're going to have

01:24:48.900 --> 01:24:50.970
prime ministers and presidents speaking.

01:24:50.970 --> 01:24:53.430
You're going to have ministries
of foreign affairs involved.

01:24:53.430 --> 01:24:55.590
And so, you have to recognize

01:24:55.590 --> 01:24:57.120
that security is part of the equation.

01:24:57.120 --> 01:24:57.953
It is.

01:24:57.953 --> 01:24:58.890
It absolutely is.

01:24:58.890 --> 01:25:02.100
So, the question is
how do we work together

01:25:02.100 --> 01:25:04.950
to solve this challenge as a public health

01:25:04.950 --> 01:25:09.060
and a national international
security issue because it is.

01:25:09.060 --> 01:25:11.577
I think COVID-19 clearly showed that.

01:25:11.577 --> 01:25:14.160
So, that is another reason
why I'm really in favor

01:25:14.160 --> 01:25:16.680
of cross-training people who
go into political sciences

01:25:16.680 --> 01:25:18.720
and end up in pandemic preparedness

01:25:18.720 --> 01:25:20.190
and people who go into public health

01:25:20.190 --> 01:25:21.900
and end up in pandemic preparedness,

01:25:21.900 --> 01:25:23.340
so they don't meet for the first time

01:25:23.340 --> 01:25:25.350
in the Situation Room at the White House.

01:25:25.350 --> 01:25:26.700
- And maybe, can I just add to that?

01:25:26.700 --> 01:25:30.300
Because I heard there
was a debate at one point

01:25:30.300 --> 01:25:31.620
and particularly when we looked

01:25:31.620 --> 01:25:34.080
at the rollout of vaccines globally,

01:25:34.080 --> 01:25:36.030
and the stark disparities that we saw,

01:25:36.030 --> 01:25:38.340
there was this kind of
rising debate that this was

01:25:38.340 --> 01:25:41.280
as a result of our securitization
of our health threat.

01:25:41.280 --> 01:25:44.820
The idea being that countries

01:25:44.820 --> 01:25:46.590
wanting to preserve their own security

01:25:46.590 --> 01:25:48.450
were therefore hoarding vaccines

01:25:48.450 --> 01:25:49.950
and not giving it to others and that

01:25:49.950 --> 01:25:53.520
had we not viewed the crisis
through the security lens,

01:25:53.520 --> 01:25:55.203
that perhaps that wouldn't exist.

01:25:56.160 --> 01:25:57.780
I will tell you, from
working in this field

01:25:57.780 --> 01:25:58.613
for more than 20 years,

01:25:58.613 --> 01:26:00.690
I fundamentally reject that interpretation

01:26:00.690 --> 01:26:03.420
because, let me tell you,
the hoarding has persisted

01:26:03.420 --> 01:26:06.570
long before we ever started
using the word health security.

01:26:06.570 --> 01:26:08.760
We saw it happen exactly in 2009.

01:26:08.760 --> 01:26:11.940
Before that was really a
term that was thrown around,

01:26:11.940 --> 01:26:13.320
countries in emergencies,

01:26:13.320 --> 01:26:15.300
particularly when they're unprepared

01:26:15.300 --> 01:26:17.970
and particularly when we don't have

01:26:17.970 --> 01:26:20.730
global mechanisms for sharing,

01:26:20.730 --> 01:26:22.560
we'll do just what we saw them do.

01:26:22.560 --> 01:26:25.530
Every single tabletop exercise
we've been in with leaders,

01:26:25.530 --> 01:26:28.320
they tend to have that tendency.

01:26:28.320 --> 01:26:30.510
So, I don't think it's the securitization.

01:26:30.510 --> 01:26:32.670
I think it is the false understanding

01:26:32.670 --> 01:26:34.860
of what it means to be secure.

01:26:34.860 --> 01:26:38.940
And being secure means,
not just looking inward,

01:26:38.940 --> 01:26:41.820
but also seeing how
events that happen outside

01:26:41.820 --> 01:26:43.383
affect what happens inward.

01:26:44.400 --> 01:26:47.187
- So, I'll take the next few
questions towards the end of,

01:26:47.187 --> 01:26:48.020
yeah, go.
- I Just wanna make

01:26:48.020 --> 01:26:48.853
one little point.

01:26:48.853 --> 01:26:51.150
It's just, there are lots of Ebola samples

01:26:51.150 --> 01:26:52.400
that are unaccounted for.

01:26:53.988 --> 01:26:56.370
So, the question of laboratory biosecurity

01:26:56.370 --> 01:26:58.920
and the transportation
of pathogens security,

01:26:58.920 --> 01:27:02.520
is of the greatest importance
and we need to upscale that.

01:27:02.520 --> 01:27:04.140
That's all I'll say.

01:27:04.140 --> 01:27:05.740
So, those who talk about

01:27:06.630 --> 01:27:08.943
biosecurity not being
an important component,

01:27:09.915 --> 01:27:11.280
(Wilmot faintly speaking)

01:27:11.280 --> 01:27:13.320
- Remember all the disaster movies start

01:27:13.320 --> 01:27:15.334
with a scientist warning about exactly

01:27:15.334 --> 01:27:17.335
this kind of
(all laughing)

01:27:17.335 --> 01:27:18.446
lots of Ebola.

01:27:18.446 --> 01:27:20.820
- You can make agents from scratch

01:27:20.820 --> 01:27:22.620
and there's very little regulation

01:27:22.620 --> 01:27:23.453
around the world--
- That is true.

01:27:23.453 --> 01:27:24.540
- about this topic.

01:27:24.540 --> 01:27:28.260
So, given that, anyone
who goes to public health

01:27:28.260 --> 01:27:30.690
needs to be thinking about

01:27:30.690 --> 01:27:33.930
diseases that are propagated
intentionally, or accidentally.

01:27:33.930 --> 01:27:35.730
That doesn't mean conspiracy theory.

01:27:35.730 --> 01:27:38.460
It means this is a fact
that you have to deal with,

01:27:38.460 --> 01:27:39.840
and that means public health and security

01:27:39.840 --> 01:27:40.830
have to work together.

01:27:40.830 --> 01:27:42.979
You will work with the security community,

01:27:42.979 --> 01:27:43.812
(device pinging)
if you're not already.

01:27:43.812 --> 01:27:46.679
So, getting comfortable
with that is important.

01:27:46.679 --> 01:27:48.180
- Yeah, so Maddie, yeah, go.

01:27:48.180 --> 01:27:50.160
- [Maddie] Hi, Beth,
this question's for you.

01:27:50.160 --> 01:27:55.160
I'm wondering if you have in
mind a country, or an entity,

01:27:56.190 --> 01:28:00.360
that has really put
together the architecture

01:28:00.360 --> 01:28:02.100
and the pieces that you're talking about.

01:28:02.100 --> 01:28:05.850
What is a model that we could work toward?

01:28:05.850 --> 01:28:07.380
Are there any?

01:28:07.380 --> 01:28:08.880
- That's a great question.

01:28:08.880 --> 01:28:09.960
I think (chuckles)

01:28:09.960 --> 01:28:12.154
a couple of us should
answer that question.

01:28:12.154 --> 01:28:12.987
I don't have one country.

01:28:12.987 --> 01:28:16.440
The model that I think
fundamentally will work,

01:28:16.440 --> 01:28:19.590
has to rely on countries being prepared,

01:28:19.590 --> 01:28:23.340
so filling the priority
gaps that they've identified

01:28:23.340 --> 01:28:25.380
through an evaluation
that either they've done,

01:28:25.380 --> 01:28:27.510
or an external organization has done.

01:28:27.510 --> 01:28:28.440
At the next layer,

01:28:28.440 --> 01:28:30.930
we need to have strong
regional institutions.

01:28:30.930 --> 01:28:33.180
I think the African
Union is a great example

01:28:33.180 --> 01:28:36.060
of a regional institution
that flexed in a crisis

01:28:36.060 --> 01:28:37.800
where they weren't able to get access.

01:28:37.800 --> 01:28:40.470
They were able to build internal tools

01:28:40.470 --> 01:28:43.020
for a continent of over 50 countries.

01:28:43.020 --> 01:28:45.060
That kind of regional
strength is important

01:28:45.060 --> 01:28:46.170
because not every country

01:28:46.170 --> 01:28:48.000
is going to be able to have everything.

01:28:48.000 --> 01:28:50.610
At the global level, we
have to have institutions

01:28:50.610 --> 01:28:54.540
that can help develop
and invest in vaccines,

01:28:54.540 --> 01:28:57.630
deliver tests and treatments
to those that can't get them.

01:28:57.630 --> 01:28:59.790
So it, it has to be at all three levels.

01:28:59.790 --> 01:29:02.340
I don't think any one country can do it.

01:29:02.340 --> 01:29:04.320
I do think that there are some countries,

01:29:04.320 --> 01:29:06.960
when you look at just how
well they work internally

01:29:06.960 --> 01:29:09.870
across sectors, that
are doing a great job.

01:29:09.870 --> 01:29:10.703
One of the reasons

01:29:10.703 --> 01:29:13.320
that the Global Health
Security Agenda was launched

01:29:13.320 --> 01:29:16.530
with a number of countries,
including Finland in 2014,

01:29:16.530 --> 01:29:19.950
is because at the time, and
I believe this is still true,

01:29:19.950 --> 01:29:21.724
the Government of Finland had

01:29:21.724 --> 01:29:23.670
a very strong interagency mentality

01:29:23.670 --> 01:29:26.280
with the ministries of health,
defense, foreign affairs,

01:29:26.280 --> 01:29:28.860
and agriculture, worked
really closely together.

01:29:28.860 --> 01:29:31.770
I think that is a model that
we need in order to be prepared

01:29:31.770 --> 01:29:34.380
for any kind of health threat
within individual governments.

01:29:34.380 --> 01:29:36.120
It's also true for the U.S. Government.

01:29:36.120 --> 01:29:38.640
I know it's not easy to
see from the outside,

01:29:38.640 --> 01:29:40.980
but there is constant communication.

01:29:40.980 --> 01:29:45.480
We just have a slightly
bigger system to hurt the cat.

01:29:45.480 --> 01:29:46.313
- So, my last...

01:29:46.313 --> 01:29:47.310
Sorry, Wilmot--
- I was gonna

01:29:47.310 --> 01:29:48.240
just add one thing.

01:29:48.240 --> 01:29:50.523
We need much better
leadership on this issue.

01:29:51.420 --> 01:29:54.480
The leaders are elected,
so you get what you get.

01:29:54.480 --> 01:29:58.920
There's no resume to evaluate,

01:29:58.920 --> 01:30:03.890
but you need, below presidents,
you need a very strong cadre

01:30:03.890 --> 01:30:06.870
of public health professionals
that provides stability

01:30:06.870 --> 01:30:09.240
in terms of leadership on
the civil service part.

01:30:09.240 --> 01:30:11.250
- [Saad] Okay, so one
last question from you.

01:30:11.250 --> 01:30:13.440
- [Audience Member]
I'm just curious to see

01:30:13.440 --> 01:30:16.470
what role can big wealthy

01:30:16.470 --> 01:30:19.710
and research grounded universities,

01:30:19.710 --> 01:30:22.380
such like Yale and Brown, play

01:30:22.380 --> 01:30:24.963
on the global stage for
pandemic preparedness?

01:30:25.860 --> 01:30:27.450
- That's a perfect question for you.

01:30:27.450 --> 01:30:28.583
- Yeah.

01:30:28.583 --> 01:30:30.033
I think a few roles.

01:30:31.080 --> 01:30:33.600
What universities do, we conduct research.

01:30:33.600 --> 01:30:36.540
We try to gather the evidence

01:30:36.540 --> 01:30:39.000
to answer the most pressing questions.

01:30:39.000 --> 01:30:43.141
We also educate, we train the
next generation of leaders

01:30:43.141 --> 01:30:45.180
(chuckles) to make sure
that we have in place

01:30:45.180 --> 01:30:47.190
exactly what Wilmot talks about.

01:30:47.190 --> 01:30:51.180
And then, one thing
that I think COVID has,

01:30:51.180 --> 01:30:53.490
I think, changed how universities operate

01:30:53.490 --> 01:30:57.000
and I hope that this is a
legacy of COVID, which is that

01:30:57.000 --> 01:30:58.920
we have to do more translational work.

01:30:58.920 --> 01:31:01.800
We have to do more work to
take the evidence and make sure

01:31:01.800 --> 01:31:03.900
it gets to the people
who are making decisions

01:31:03.900 --> 01:31:06.240
and continue to push on them
to make the right decisions

01:31:06.240 --> 01:31:08.100
based on the evidence.

01:31:08.100 --> 01:31:12.330
That piece, I think
there will always be room

01:31:12.330 --> 01:31:16.350
for independent credible
voices to both evaluate

01:31:16.350 --> 01:31:20.040
what governments are saying,
as well as continue to educate,

01:31:20.040 --> 01:31:22.740
not just people in our
programs, but the general public

01:31:22.740 --> 01:31:24.260
for how they can protect themselves.

01:31:24.260 --> 01:31:27.840
So, I think that translational
piece will be, hopefully,

01:31:27.840 --> 01:31:32.013
a bigger piece of the portfolio
of what universities do.

01:31:33.270 --> 01:31:38.220
- Yeah, no, I think that's
a good place to end.

01:31:38.220 --> 01:31:40.447
I resisted rephrasing that question as,

01:31:40.447 --> 01:31:42.039
"What can Brown do for you?"

01:31:42.039 --> 01:31:44.622
(all laughing)

01:31:45.863 --> 01:31:49.140
but thank you all for being here

01:31:49.140 --> 01:31:51.807
for your insightful responses.

01:31:51.807 --> 01:31:54.417
And thank you for you all for being here

01:31:54.417 --> 01:31:59.417
and for your questions,
both from within this room

01:31:59.760 --> 01:32:00.990
and online.

01:32:00.990 --> 01:32:05.142
So, with that, we end this panel.

01:32:05.142 --> 01:32:10.142
Thank you.
(audience applauding)

01:32:11.411 --> 01:32:15.078
(whimsical music)

